{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05046","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05046","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05046","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Beauchemin","name":"Beauchemin, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Tremblay","name":"Tremblay, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed Amine","familyName":"Youssef","name":"Youssef, Mohamed Amine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Khoury","name":"Khoury, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COLE: a Comprehensive Benchmark for French Language Understanding Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:26:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:47:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T02:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To address the need for a more comprehensive evaluation of French Natural Language Understanding (NLU), we introduce COLE, a new benchmark composed of 23 diverse task covering a broad range of NLU capabilities, including sentiment analysis, paraphrase detection, grammatical judgment, and reasoning, with a particular focus on linguistic phenomena relevant to the French language. We benchmark 94 large language models (LLM), providing an extensive analysis of the current state of French NLU. Our results highlight a significant performance gap between closed- and open-weights models and identify key challenging frontiers for current LLMs, such as zero-shot extractive question-answering (QA), fine-grained word sense disambiguation, and understanding of regional language variations. We release COLE as a public resource to foster further progress in French language modelling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to ACL Rolling Review of October"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05046","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:27:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06217","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06217","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06217","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaru","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Jiaru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumya","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Soumya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinay Kumar","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Vinay Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Wipf","name":"Wipf, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sumit","familyName":"Negi","name":"Negi, Sumit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingrui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jingrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TaTToo: Tool-Grounded Thinking PRM for Test-Time Scaling in Tabular Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:04:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Process Reward Models (PRMs) have recently emerged as a powerful framework for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of large reasoning models (LRMs), particularly in the context of test-time scaling (TTS). However, their potential for supervising LRMs on tabular reasoning domains remains underexplored. Through detailed empirical analyses, we identify that existing PRMs, though widely adopted for supervising text-only reasoning steps, struggle with table-specific operations such as sub-table retrieval and schema interaction, leading to critical performance bottlenecks. To address this limitation, we propose TaTToo, a novel table-grounded PRM framework that (i) reasons explicitly over tabular reasoning steps and (ii) integrates tool-based verification to provide precise reward supervision. Concretely, we first design a scalable data curation pipeline that constructs over 60k high-quality step-level annotations by integrating table verification rationales with tool-based executions. Building on the collected data, we train TaTToo with a dual-stage paradigm: cold-start supervised fine-tuning to capture tool-use reasoning patterns, followed by reinforcement learning with tool-grounded reward shaping to align our model with table-based verification. We provide a comprehensive evaluation of the policy improvement induced by our newly designed PRM. Across 5 challenging tabular reasoning benchmarks covering numerical reasoning, fact-checking, and data analysis, TaTToo improves downstream policy LRMs by 30.9% at inference, surpasses strong PRM baselines such as Qwen-2.5-Math-PRM-72B with only 8B parameters, and demonstrates strong generalizability across diverse TTS strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06217","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:07:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06214","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06214","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06214","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Mingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengshuang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hengshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaya","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jiaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stratified GRPO: Handling Structural Heterogeneity in Reinforcement Learning of LLM Search Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:03:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly rely on external tools such as search engines to solve complex, multi-step problems, and reinforcement learning (RL) has become a key paradigm for training them. However, the trajectories of search agents are structurally heterogeneous, where variations in the number, placement, and outcomes of search calls lead to fundamentally different answer directions and reward distributions. Standard policy gradient methods, which use a single global baseline, suffer from what we identify and formalize as cross-stratum bias-an \"apples-to-oranges\" comparison of heterogeneous trajectories. This cross-stratum bias distorts credit assignment and hinders exploration of complex, multi-step search strategies. To address this, we propose Stratified GRPO, whose central component, Stratified Advantage Normalization (SAN), partitions trajectories into homogeneous strata based on their structural properties and computes advantages locally within each stratum. This ensures that trajectories are evaluated only against their true peers. Our analysis proves that SAN eliminates cross-stratum bias, yields conditionally unbiased unit-variance estimates inside each stratum, and retains the global unbiasedness and unit-variance properties enjoyed by standard normalization, resulting in a more pure and scale-stable learning signal. To improve practical stability under finite-sample regimes, we further linearly blend SAN with the global estimator. Extensive experiments on diverse single-hop and multi-hop question-answering benchmarks demonstrate that Stratified GRPO consistently and substantially outperforms GRPO by up to 11.3 points, achieving higher training rewards, greater training stability, and more effective search policies. These results establish stratification as a principled remedy for structural heterogeneity in RL for LLM search agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06214","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:07:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06179","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06179","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06179","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emre","familyName":"Adabag","name":"Adabag, Emre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Greiff","name":"Greiff, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Subosits","name":"Subosits, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Lew","name":"Lew, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differentiable Model Predictive Control on the GPU"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:42:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Differentiable model predictive control (MPC) offers a powerful framework for combining learning and control. However, its adoption has been limited by the inherently sequential nature of traditional optimization algorithms, which are challenging to parallelize on modern computing hardware like GPUs. In this work, we tackle this bottleneck by introducing a GPU-accelerated differentiable optimization tool for MPC. This solver leverages sequential quadratic programming and a custom preconditioned conjugate gradient (PCG) routine with tridiagonal preconditioning to exploit the problem's structure and enable efficient parallelization. We demonstrate substantial speedups over CPU- and GPU-based baselines, significantly improving upon state-of-the-art training times on benchmark reinforcement learning and imitation learning tasks. Finally, we showcase the method on the challenging task of reinforcement learning for driving at the limits of handling, where it enables robust drifting of a Toyota Supra through water puddles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06179","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Dingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VecInfer: Efficient LLM Inference with Low-Bit KV Cache via Outlier-Suppressed Vector Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Key-Value (KV) cache introduces substantial memory overhead during large language model (LLM) inference. Although existing vector quantization (VQ) methods reduce KV cache usage and provide flexible representational capacity across bit-widths, they suffer severe performance degradation at ultra-low bit-widths due to key cache outliers that hinder effective codebook utilization. To address this challenge, we propose VecInfer, a novel VQ method for aggressive KV cache compression while enabling efficient inference. By applying smooth and Hadamard transformations, VecInfer suppresses outliers in the key cache, enabling the codebook to comprehensively cover the original data distribution and thereby reducing quantization difficulty. To facilitate efficient deployment, we design an optimized CUDA kernel that fuses computation with dequantization to minimize memory access overhead. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that VecInfer consistently outperforms existing quantization baselines across both long-context understanding and mathematical reasoning tasks. With only 2-bit quantization, VecInfer achieves performance comparable to full precision, while delivering up to $\\mathbf{2.7\\times}$ speedup in large-batch self-attention computation and $\\mathbf{8.3\\times}$ reduction in single-batch end-to-end latency on Llama-3.1-8B with a 196k sequence length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aju Ani","familyName":"Justus","name":"Justus, Aju Ani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Baber","name":"Baber, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLMs as Policy-Agnostic Teammates: A Case Study in Human Proxy Design for Heterogeneous Agent Teams"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:21:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A critical challenge in modelling Heterogeneous-Agent Teams is training agents to collaborate with teammates whose policies are inaccessible or non-stationary, such as humans. Traditional approaches rely on expensive human-in-the-loop data, which limits scalability. We propose using Large Language Models (LLMs) as policy-agnostic human proxies to generate synthetic data that mimics human decision-making. To evaluate this, we conduct three experiments in a grid-world capture game inspired by Stag Hunt, a game theory paradigm that balances risk and reward. In Experiment 1, we compare decisions from 30 human participants and 2 expert judges with outputs from LLaMA 3.1 and Mixtral 8x22B models. LLMs, prompted with game-state observations and reward structures, align more closely with experts than participants, demonstrating consistency in applying underlying decision criteria. Experiment 2 modifies prompts to induce risk-sensitive strategies (e.g. \"be risk averse\"). LLM outputs mirror human participants' variability, shifting between risk-averse and risk-seeking behaviours. Finally, Experiment 3 tests LLMs in a dynamic grid-world where the LLM agents generate movement actions. LLMs produce trajectories resembling human participants' paths. While LLMs cannot yet fully replicate human adaptability, their prompt-guided diversity offers a scalable foundation for simulating policy-agnostic teammates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is a preprint of a paper presented at the \\textit{European Conference on Artificial Intelligence (ECAI 2025)}. It is made publicly available for the benefit of the research community and should be regarded as a preprint rather than a formally reviewed publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06149","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06149","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06149","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hwanwoo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hwanwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongkyu Derek","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Dongkyu Derek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Laber","name":"Laber, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Implicit Updates for Average-Reward Temporal Difference Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:19:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Temporal difference (TD) learning is a cornerstone of reinforcement learning. In the average-reward setting, standard TD($λ$) is highly sensitive to the choice of step-size and thus requires careful tuning to maintain numerical stability. We introduce average-reward implicit TD($λ$), which employs an implicit fixed point update to provide data-adaptive stabilization while preserving the per iteration computational complexity of standard average-reward TD($λ$). In contrast to prior finite-time analyses of average-reward TD($λ$), which impose restrictive step-size conditions, we establish finite-time error bounds for the implicit variant under substantially weaker step-size requirements. Empirically, average-reward implicit TD($λ$) operates reliably over a much broader range of step-sizes and exhibits markedly improved numerical stability. This enables more efficient policy evaluation and policy learning, highlighting its effectiveness as a robust alternative to average-reward TD($λ$)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06149","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:31Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06138","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06138","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06138","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rushiv","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Rushiv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Task Reinforcement Learning with Language-Encoded Gated Policy Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:12:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-task reinforcement learning often relies on task metadata -- such as brief natural-language descriptions -- to guide behavior across diverse objectives. We present Lexical Policy Networks (LEXPOL), a language-conditioned mixture-of-policies architecture for multi-task RL. LEXPOL encodes task metadata with a text encoder and uses a learned gating module to select or blend among multiple sub-policies, enabling end-to-end training across tasks. On MetaWorld benchmarks, LEXPOL matches or exceeds strong multi-task baselines in success rate and sample efficiency, without task-specific retraining. To analyze the mechanism, we further study settings with fixed expert policies obtained independently of the gate and show that the learned language gate composes these experts to produce behaviors appropriate to novel task descriptions and unseen task combinations. These results indicate that natural-language metadata can effectively index and recombine reusable skills within a single policy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 3 figures, 12 tables, 2 appendices. Currently under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06138","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weihao","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Weihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keqing","familyName":"He","name":"He, Keqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuqiao","familyName":"Kuang","name":"Kuang, Chuqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoguang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xiaoguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxian","familyName":"He","name":"He, Junxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pushing Test-Time Scaling Limits of Deep Search with Asymmetric Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:09:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time compute can be scaled both sequentially and in parallel. Sequential scaling involves lengthening the generation process, while parallel scaling involves verifying and selecting among multiple candidate outputs. Combining these two strategies has led to the most powerful AI systems, such as Grok 4 Heavy and GPT-5 Pro. In certain contexts (e.g., solving Sudoku puzzles), verifying responses can be substantially easier than generating them. This property, referred to as \\emph{asymmetric verification}, highlights the strong potential of test-time scaling (TTS). In this work, we study both sequential and parallel TTS of deep search agents, motivated by the intuition that verification in this setting is often much easier than generation. In experiments, we first show that sequential scaling methods, such as budget forcing, can be effective initially but soon degrade performance. Leveraging asymmetric verification, however, we are able to achieve substantial improvements by allocating only a modest amount of compute to the verifier. We conduct experiments with flagship open-source models and extend them to their ``Heavy'' variants through TTS. These deep research agents achieve gains of up to 27 absolute points on benchmarks such as BrowseComp. Remarkably, as an open-source alternative, GLM-4.5 Heavy reaches accuracy of {\\bf 54.0\\%} on BrowseComp and {\\bf 66.0\\%} on GAIA, placing it comparable to the best proprietary choices such as OpenAI Deep Research. Tongyi-DeepResearch Heavy further achieves {\\bf 69.0\\%} accuracy on BrowseComp, greatly surpassing the best proprietary results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06131","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06131","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06131","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangqiong","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Liangqiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daguang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Daguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuyin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Discrete Diffusion Models with MLLMs for Unified Medical Multimodal Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:06:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:00:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in generative medical models are constrained by modality-specific scenarios that hinder the integration of complementary evidence from imaging, pathology, and clinical notes. This fragmentation limits their evolution into foundation models that can learn and reason across the full spectrum of biomedical data. We propose MeDiM, the first medical discrete diffusion model that learns shared distributions across modalities without modality-specific components. MeDiM unifies multiple generative tasks: translating between images and text, and jointly producing image-report pairs across domains in response to prompts. Built on a discrete diffusion framework, MeDiM bridges vision and language representations through a shared probabilistic space. To enable unified and flexible medical generation, we employ a multimodal large language model (MLLM) as the diffusion backbone, leveraging its prior knowledge and cross-modal reasoning. Two key designs are introduced: (1) removing the causal attention mask for bidirectional context, and (2) injecting continuous timestep embeddings for diffusion awareness. Experiments demonstrate high-fidelity medical generation (FID 16.60 on MIMIC-CXR and FID 24.19 on PathGen) and accurate report generation (METEOR 0.2650 and 0.2580). Jointly generated image-report pairs further enhance downstream performance (plus6.43 percent BLEU-1, plus18.57 percent BLEU-2, plus31.58 percent BLEU-3, plus4.80 percent METEOR), showing that MeDiM supports coherent and clinically grounded multimodal outputs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages,6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06131","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:05:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muyu","familyName":"He","name":"He, Muyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Ali","familyName":"Shafique","name":"Shafique, Muhammad Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anand","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anand","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tsach","familyName":"Mackey","name":"Mackey, Tsach","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nazneen","familyName":"Rajani","name":"Rajani, Nazneen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Valley of Code Reasoning: Scaling Knowledge Distillation of Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:32:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:58:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distilling the thinking traces of a Large Language Model (LLM) with reasoning capabilities into a smaller model has been proven effective. Yet, there is a scarcity of work done on how model performances scale with the quantity of distillation data. In this work, we study the scaling trend of distilling competitive coding skills on two small non-reasoning LLMs. We validate the hypothesis that there is a $\\textit{valley of code reasoning}$: downstream performance on competitive coding first drops as data quantity increases, then it steadily increases in a sharper-than-log-linear fashion. Having identified the trend, we further fine-tune the models at two different distillation stages on the same data to ground conclusions on their respective learning phases. We learn that across stages in the low and medium-low data regimes, small models benefit significantly from easier coding questions than from harder ones. We also find that, surprisingly, the correctness of outputs in training data makes no difference to distillation outcomes. Our work represents a step forward in understanding the training dynamics of code reasoning distillation outside intuition"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Deep Learning for Code (DL4C), Project page: https://collinear.ai/valley-of-reasoning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:04:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhe","name":"Zhe, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fateme","familyName":"Memar","name":"Memar, Fateme","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongren","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhongren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dongjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Constraint-Aware Route Recommendation from Natural Language via Hierarchical LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:03:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:57:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Route recommendation aims to provide users with optimal travel plans that satisfy diverse and complex requirements. Classical routing algorithms (e.g., shortest-path and constraint-aware search) are efficient but assume structured inputs and fixed objectives, limiting adaptability to natural-language queries. Recent LLM-based approaches enhance flexibility but struggle with spatial reasoning and the joint modeling of route-level and POI-level preferences. To address these limitations, we propose RouteLLM, a hierarchical multi-agent framework that grounds natural-language intents into constraint-aware routes. It first parses user queries into structured intents including POIs, paths, and constraints. A manager agent then coordinates specialized sub-agents: a constraint agent that resolves and formally check constraints, a POI agent that retrieves and ranks candidate POIs, and a path refinement agent that refines routes via a routing engine with preference-conditioned costs. A final verifier agent ensures constraint satisfaction and produces the final route with an interpretable rationale. This design bridges linguistic flexibility and spatial structure, enabling reasoning over route feasibility and user preferences. Experiments show that our method reliably grounds textual preferences into constraint-aware routes, improving route quality and preference satisfaction over classical methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06077","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06077","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06077","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mi","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Mi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihui","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Zihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Dimakis","name":"Dimakis, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kristen","familyName":"Grauman","name":"Grauman, Kristen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"When Thinking Drifts: Evidential Grounding for Robust Video Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T16:03:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Video reasoning, the task of enabling machines to infer from dynamic visual content through multi-step logic, is crucial for advanced AI. While the Chain-of-Thought (CoT) mechanism has enhanced reasoning in text-based tasks, its application to video understanding remains underexplored. This paper presents a systematic analysis revealing that CoT often degrades performance in video reasoning, generating verbose but misleading internal monologues, and leading to hallucinated visual details and overridden correct intuitions - a phenomenon we term \"visual thinking drift\". We explain this drift through a Bayesian lens, positing that CoT traces often diverge from actual visual evidence, instead amplifying internal biases or language priors, causing models to storytell rather than engage in grounded reasoning. To counteract this, we introduce Visual Evidence Reward (VER), a novel reinforcement learning framework that explicitly rewards the generation of reasoning traces that are verifiably grounded in visual evidence. Comprehensive evaluation across 10 diverse video understanding benchmarks demonstrates that our Video-VER consistently achieves top performance. Our work sheds light on the distinct challenges of video-centric reasoning and encourages the development of AI that robustly grounds its inferences in visual evidence - for large multimodal models that not only \"think before answering\", but also \"see while thinking\"."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025, Project page: https://vision.cs.utexas.edu/projects/video-ver/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06077","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"João","familyName":"Palmeiro","name":"Palmeiro, João","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diogo","familyName":"Duarte","name":"Duarte, Diogo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rita","familyName":"Costa","name":"Costa, Rita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Bizarro","name":"Bizarro, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmark It Yourself (BIY): Preparing a Dataset and Benchmarking AI Models for Scatterplot-Related Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI models are increasingly used for data analysis and visualization, yet benchmarks rarely address scatterplot-specific tasks, limiting insight into performance. To address this gap for one of the most common chart types, we introduce a synthetic, annotated dataset of over 18,000 scatterplots from six data generators and 17 chart designs, and a benchmark based on it. We evaluate proprietary models from OpenAI and Google using N-shot prompting on five distinct tasks derived from annotations of cluster bounding boxes, their center coordinates, and outlier coordinates. OpenAI models and Gemini 2.5 Flash, especially when prompted with examples, are viable options for counting clusters and, in Flash's case, outliers (90%+ Accuracy). However, the results for localization-related tasks are unsatisfactory: Precision and Recall are near or below 50%, except for Flash in outlier identification (65.01%). Furthermore, the impact of chart design on performance appears to be a secondary factor, but it is advisable to avoid scatterplots with wide aspect ratios (16:9 and 21:9) or those colored randomly. Supplementary materials are available at https://github.com/feedzai/biy-paper."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures, short paper accepted at VISxGenAI: 1st Workshop on GenAI, Agents, and the Future of VIS (IEEE VIS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:42Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06056","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06056","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06056","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scientific Algorithm Discovery by Augmenting AlphaEvolve with Deep Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:49:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models hold promise as scientific assistants, yet existing agents either rely solely on algorithm evolution or on deep research in isolation, both of which face critical limitations. Pure algorithm evolution, as in AlphaEvolve, depends only on the internal knowledge of LLMs and quickly plateaus in complex domains, while pure deep research proposes ideas without validation, resulting in unrealistic or unimplementable solutions. We present DeepEvolve, an agent that integrates deep research with algorithm evolution, uniting external knowledge retrieval, cross-file code editing, and systematic debugging under a feedback-driven iterative loop. Each iteration not only proposes new hypotheses but also refines, implements, and tests them, avoiding both shallow improvements and unproductive over-refinements. Across nine benchmarks in chemistry, mathematics, biology, materials, and patents, DeepEvolve consistently improves the initial algorithm, producing executable new algorithms with sustained gains. By bridging the gap between unguided evolution and research without grounding, DeepEvolve provides a reliable framework for advancing scientific algorithm discovery. Our code is available at https://github.com/liugangcode/deepevolve."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 17 figures, 4 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06056","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinye","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Xinye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongcan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hongcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoshun","familyName":"Nan","name":"Nan, Guoshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaojuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VideoMiner: Iteratively Grounding Key Frames of Hour-Long Videos via Tree-based Group Relative Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:34:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding hour-long videos with multi-modal large language models (MM-LLMs) enriches the landscape of human-centered AI applications. However, for end-to-end video understanding with LLMs, uniformly sampling video frames results in LLMs being overwhelmed by a vast amount of irrelevant information as video length increases. Existing hierarchical key frame extraction methods improve the accuracy of video understanding but still face two critical challenges. 1) How can the interference of extensive redundant information in long videos be mitigated? 2) How can a model dynamically adapt to complex hierarchical structures while accurately identifying key frames? To address these issues, we propose VideoMiner, which iteratively segments, captions, and clusters long videos, forming a hierarchical tree structure. The proposed VideoMiner progresses from long videos to events to frames while preserving temporal coherence, effectively addressing the first challenge. To precisely locate key frames, we introduce T-GRPO, a tree-based group relative policy optimization in reinforcement learning method that guides the exploration of the VideoMiner. The proposed T-GRPO is specifically designed for tree structures, integrating spatiotemporal information at the event level while being guided by the question, thus solving the second challenge. We achieve superior performance in all long-video understanding tasks and uncover several interesting insights. Our proposed T-GRPO surprisingly incentivizes the model to spontaneously generate a reasoning chain. Additionally, the designed tree growth auxin dynamically adjusts the expansion depth, obtaining accuracy and efficiency gains. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/caoxinye/VideoMiner."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICCV 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06038","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06038","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06038","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zeqiao","name":"Zeqiao, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Yijing","name":"Yijing, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Haoyu","name":"Haoyu, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Wenfei","name":"Wenfei, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zuo","familyName":"Zhiqiang","name":"Zhiqiang, Zuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Learning to Mastery: Achieving Safe and Efficient Real-World Autonomous Driving with Human-In-The-Loop Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:33:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous driving with reinforcement learning (RL) has significant potential. However, applying RL in real-world settings remains challenging due to the need for safe, efficient, and robust learning. Incorporating human expertise into the learning process can help overcome these challenges by reducing risky exploration and improving sample efficiency. In this work, we propose a reward-free, active human-in-the-loop learning method called Human-Guided Distributional Soft Actor-Critic (H-DSAC). Our method combines Proxy Value Propagation (PVP) and Distributional Soft Actor-Critic (DSAC) to enable efficient and safe training in real-world environments. The key innovation is the construction of a distributed proxy value function within the DSAC framework. This function encodes human intent by assigning higher expected returns to expert demonstrations and penalizing actions that require human intervention. By extrapolating these labels to unlabeled states, the policy is effectively guided toward expert-like behavior. With a well-designed state space, our method achieves real-world driving policy learning within practical training times. Results from both simulation and real-world experiments demonstrate that our framework enables safe, robust, and sample-efficient learning for autonomous driving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06038","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06036","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06036","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06036","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Qingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chak Tou","familyName":"Leong","name":"Leong, Chak Tou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Linyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiting","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaehong","familyName":"Yoon","name":"Yoon, Jaehong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"YunXing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"XingYu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinjin","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Jinjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Refusal Falls off a Cliff: How Safety Alignment Fails in Reasoning?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:32:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large reasoning models (LRMs) with multi-step reasoning capabilities have shown remarkable problem-solving abilities, yet they exhibit concerning safety vulnerabilities that remain poorly understood. In this work, we investigate why safety alignment fails in reasoning models through a mechanistic interpretability lens. Using a linear probing approach to trace refusal intentions across token positions, we discover a striking phenomenon termed as \\textbf{refusal cliff}: many poorly-aligned reasoning models correctly identify harmful prompts and maintain strong refusal intentions during their thinking process, but experience a sharp drop in refusal scores at the final tokens before output generation. This suggests that these models are not inherently unsafe; rather, their refusal intentions are systematically suppressed. Through causal intervention analysis, we identify a sparse set of attention heads that negatively contribute to refusal behavior. Ablating just 3\\% of these heads can reduce attack success rates below 10\\%. Building on these mechanistic insights, we propose \\textbf{Cliff-as-a-Judge}, a novel data selection method that identifies training examples exhibiting the largest refusal cliff to efficiently repair reasoning models' safety alignment. This approach achieves comparable safety improvements using only 1.7\\% of the vanilla safety training data, demonstrating a less-is-more effect in safety alignment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06036","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:53Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06014","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06014","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06014","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhangyue","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Zhangyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiushi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qiushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qipeng","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xuanjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xipeng","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARISE: An Adaptive Resolution-Aware Metric for Test-Time Scaling Evaluation in Large Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:10:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:54:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time scaling has emerged as a transformative paradigm for enhancing the performance of large reasoning models, enabling dynamic allocation of computational resources during inference. However, as the landscape of reasoning models rapidly expands, a critical question remains: how can we systematically compare and evaluate the test-time scaling capabilities across different models? In this paper, we introduce ARISE (Adaptive Resolution-aware Scaling Evaluation), a novel metric specifically designed to assess the test-time scaling effectiveness of large reasoning models. Unlike existing evaluation approaches, ARISE incorporates two key innovations: (1) sample-level awareness that effectively penalizes negative scaling behaviors where increased computation leads to performance degradation, and (2) a dynamic sampling mechanism that mitigates the impact of accuracy fluctuations and token count instability on the final assessment. We conduct comprehensive experiments evaluating state-of-the-art reasoning models across diverse domains including mathematical reasoning, code generation, and agentic tasks. Our results demonstrate that ARISE provides a reliable and fine-grained measurement of test-time scaling capabilities, revealing significant variations in scaling efficiency across models. Notably, our evaluation identifies Claude Opus as exhibiting superior scaling characteristics compared to other contemporary reasoning models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06014","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aueaphum","familyName":"Aueawatthanaphisut","name":"Aueawatthanaphisut, Aueaphum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nyi Wunna","familyName":"Tun","name":"Tun, Nyi Wunna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Quantum-Classical Policy Gradient for Adaptive Control of Cyber-Physical Systems: A Comparative Study of VQC vs. MLP"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:09:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The comparative evaluation between classical and quantum reinforcement learning (QRL) paradigms was conducted to investigate their convergence behavior, robustness under observational noise, and computational efficiency in a benchmark control environment. The study employed a multilayer perceptron (MLP) agent as a classical baseline and a parameterized variational quantum circuit (VQC) as a quantum counterpart, both trained on the CartPole-v1 environment over 500 episodes. Empirical results demonstrated that the classical MLP achieved near-optimal policy convergence with a mean return of 498.7 +/- 3.2, maintaining stable equilibrium throughout training. In contrast, the VQC exhibited limited learning capability, with an average return of 14.6 +/- 4.8, primarily constrained by circuit depth and qubit connectivity. Noise robustness analysis further revealed that the MLP policy deteriorated gracefully under Gaussian perturbations, while the VQC displayed higher sensitivity at equivalent noise levels. Despite the lower asymptotic performance, the VQC exhibited significantly lower parameter count and marginally increased training time, highlighting its potential scalability for low-resource quantum processors. The results suggest that while classical neural policies remain dominant in current control benchmarks, quantum-enhanced architectures could offer promising efficiency advantages once hardware noise and expressivity limitations are mitigated."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 5 figures, 2 tables, 17 equations, 1 algorithm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:02:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:02:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:02:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05996","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05996","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05996","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moritz","familyName":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Moritz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Krug","name":"Krug, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narunas","familyName":"Vaskevicius","name":"Vaskevicius, Narunas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luigi","familyName":"Palmieri","name":"Palmieri, Luigi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Volpp","name":"Volpp, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joschka","familyName":"Boedecker","name":"Boedecker, Joschka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Information-Theoretic Policy Pre-Training with Empowerment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:57:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:53:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Empowerment, an information-theoretic measure of an agent's potential influence on its environment, has emerged as a powerful intrinsic motivation and exploration framework for reinforcement learning (RL). Besides for unsupervised RL and skill learning algorithms, the specific use of empowerment as a pre-training signal has received limited attention in the literature. We show that empowerment can be used as a pre-training signal for data-efficient downstream task adaptation. For this we extend the traditional notion of empowerment by introducing discounted empowerment, which balances the agent's control over the environment across short- and long-term horizons. Leveraging this formulation, we propose a novel pre-training paradigm that initializes policies to maximize discounted empowerment, enabling agents to acquire a robust understanding of environmental dynamics. We analyze empowerment-based pre-training for various existing RL algorithms and empirically demonstrate its potential as a general-purpose initialization strategy: empowerment-maximizing policies with long horizons are data-efficient and effective, leading to improved adaptability in downstream tasks. Our findings pave the way for future research to scale this framework to high-dimensional and complex tasks, further advancing the field of RL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05996","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05987","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05987","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05987","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueyan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xueyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guinan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Guinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmaya","familyName":"Sachan","name":"Sachan, Mrinmaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Geiping","name":"Geiping, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sample Smart, Not Hard: Correctness-First Decoding for Better Reasoning in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:46:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to complex tasks that require extended reasoning. In such settings, models often benefit from diverse chains-of-thought to arrive at multiple candidate solutions. This requires two competing objectives: to inject enough stochasticity to explore multiple reasoning chains, and to ensure sufficient accuracy and quality in each path. Existing works pursue the first objective by increasing exploration at highly uncertain steps with higher temperature or larger candidate token sets, while others improve reliability by rejecting samples with low confidence post-generation, implying that low confidence correlates with low answer quality. These two lines of thought are in conflict, as they conflate different sources of uncertainty. To resolve this, we argue that the decoding rule should be calibrated by correctness, not confidence alone. We should sample from tokens with higher estimated correctness, and reduce sampling where expected correctness is low. We propose simple strategies that achieve this goal: Greedy-Threshold makes sampling greedy at very low confidence steps. Calibrated-TopK and Calibrated-epsilon set truncation threshold based on estimated rank-wise correctness. Together, our findings challenge prevailing heuristics about decoding under uncertainty and show gains across math and general reasoning benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05987","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:44Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05972","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05972","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05972","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Periklis","familyName":"Mantenoglou","name":"Mantenoglou, Periklis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi","familyName":"Hazra","name":"Hazra, Rishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro Zuidberg Dos","familyName":"Martires","name":"Martires, Pedro Zuidberg Dos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luc","familyName":"De Raedt","name":"De Raedt, Luc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LexiCon: a Benchmark for Planning under Temporal Constraints in Natural Language"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:28:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Owing to their reasoning capabilities, large language models (LLMs) have been evaluated on planning tasks described in natural language. However, LLMs have largely been tested on planning domains without constraints. In order to deploy them in real-world settings where adherence to constraints, in particular safety constraints, is critical, we need to evaluate their performance on constrained planning tasks. We introduce LexiCon -- a natural language-based (Lexi) constrained (Con) planning benchmark, consisting of a suite of environments, that can be used to evaluate the planning capabilities of LLMs in a principled fashion. The core idea behind LexiCon is to take existing planning environments and impose temporal constraints on the states. These constrained problems are then translated into natural language and given to an LLM to solve. A key feature of LexiCon is its extensibility. That is, the set of supported environments can be extended with new (unconstrained) environment generators, for which temporal constraints are constructed automatically. This renders LexiCon future-proof: the hardness of the generated planning problems can be increased as the planning capabilities of LLMs improve. Our experiments reveal that the performance of state-of-the-art LLMs, including reasoning models like GPT-5, o3, and R1, deteriorates as the degree of constrainedness of the planning tasks increases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05972","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05962","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05962","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05962","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayyán","familyName":"O'Brien","name":"O'Brien, Dayyán","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Barry","familyName":"Haddow","name":"Haddow, Barry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Allaway","name":"Allaway, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pinzhen","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Pinzhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MatheMagic: Generating Dynamic Mathematics Benchmarks Robust to Memorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conducting contamination-free evaluation of mathematical capabilities can be difficult for two reasons: models may memorize a test set once it is made public, and current mathematical benchmarks are prone to overfitting due to having limited diversity of symbols and rules, coupled with closed-ended answers. This paper proposes a method to leverage these shortcomings as useful features to a construct dynamic, counterfactual benchmark, which can be used to both reveal overfitting and measure true reasoning. We demonstrate this via MatheMagic, which generates math test instances with the interpretations of numbers and operators altered, yet has automatically verifiable answers. Test instances are randomly seeded and constructed at test time to evaluate a model's induction or deduction capability, offering stability, extensibility, comparability, and robustness to overfitting. Our experiments find that models solve deduction more easily than induction, but they revert to standard math. Further analysis reveals that math-adapted models fail to exhibit a general \"skill\" of reasoning, and fine-tuning on induction tasks generalizes poorly."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05962","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:08Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05957","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05957","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05957","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaughn","familyName":"Gzenda","name":"Gzenda, Vaughn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Chhabra","name":"Chhabra, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Crawl: Latent Model-Based Reinforcement Learning for Soft Robotic Adaptive Locomotion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:14:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:50:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Soft robotic crawlers are mobile robots that utilize soft body deformability and compliance to achieve locomotion through surface contact. Designing control strategies for such systems is challenging due to model inaccuracies, sensor noise, and the need to discover locomotor gaits. In this work, we present a model-based reinforcement learning (MB-RL) framework in which latent dynamics inferred from onboard sensors serve as a predictive model that guides an actor-critic algorithm to optimize locomotor policies. We evaluate the framework on a minimal crawler model in simulation using inertial measurement units and time-of-flight sensors as observations. The learned latent dynamics enable short-horizon motion prediction while the actor-critic discovers effective locomotor policies. This approach highlights the potential of latent-dynamics MB-RL for enabling embodied soft robotic adaptive locomotion based solely on noisy sensor feedback."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05957","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:01Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyue","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zheyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Abdullahi","name":"Abdullahi, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuo","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huining","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Huining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zelai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Boxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EARL: Efficient Agentic Reinforcement Learning Systems for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:52:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:49:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a pivotal component of large language model (LLM) post-training, and agentic RL extends this paradigm to operate as agents through multi-turn interaction and tool use. Scaling such systems exposes two practical bottlenecks: (1) context length grows rapidly during training, inflating memory usage and latency, and triggering out-of-memory (OOM) failures; and (2) intermediate tensors accumulate with context length, making cross-device data movement a major system bottleneck.\n We present EARL, a scalable system for efficient agentic RL. EARL designs a parallelism selector that dynamically adapts model and training parallelism across RL stages based on sequence length and system load, and a data dispatcher that performs layout-aware, decentralized exchange of intermediate data batches. Together, these components increase throughput, reduce long-context failures, and enable stable large-scale training of agentic LLMs without relying on hard limits or penalties of context length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:00:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05876","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05876","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05876","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhimanyu","familyName":"Choudhury","name":"Choudhury, Abhimanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meena","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Meena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Interplay of Cube Learning and Dependency Schemes in QCDCL Proof Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Logic in Computer Science (cs.LO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Complexity (cs.CC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:45:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:45:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantified Conflict Driven Clause Leaning (QCDCL) is one of the main approaches to solving Quantified Boolean Formulas (QBF). Cube-learning is employed in this approach to ensure that true formulas can be verified. Dependency Schemes help to detect spurious dependencies that are implied by the variable ordering in the quantifier prefix of QBFs but are not essential for constructing (counter)models. This detection can provably shorten refutations in specific proof systems, and is expected to speed up runs of QBF solvers.\n The simplest underlying proof system [BeyersdorffBöhm-LMCS2023], formalises the reasoning in the QCDCL approach on false formulas, when neither cube learning nor dependency schemes is used. The work of [BöhmPeitlBeyersdorff-AI2024] further incorporates cube-learning. The work of [ChoudhuryMahajan-JAR2024] incorporates a limited use of dependency schemes, but without cube-learning.\n In this work, proof systems underlying the reasoning of QCDCL solvers which use cube learning, and which use dependency schemes at all stages, are formalised. Sufficient conditions for soundness and completeness are presented, and it is shown that using the standard and reflexive resolution path dependency schemes ($D^{std}$ and $D^{rrs}$) to relax the decision order provably shortens refutations.\n When the decisions are restricted to follow quantification order, but dependency schemes are used in propagation and learning, in conjunction with cube-learning, the resulting proof systems using the dependency schemes $D^{std}$ and $D^{rrs}$ are investigated in detail and their relative strengths are analysed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"30 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05876","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:59:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05871","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05871","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05871","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josefa Lia","familyName":"Stoisser","name":"Stoisser, Josefa Lia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lawrence","familyName":"Phillips","name":"Phillips, Lawrence","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Misra","name":"Misra, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tom A.","familyName":"Lamb","name":"Lamb, Tom A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Torr","name":"Torr, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc Boubnovski","familyName":"Martell","name":"Martell, Marc Boubnovski","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Julien","familyName":"Fauqueur","name":"Fauqueur, Julien","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaspar","familyName":"Märtens","name":"Märtens, Kaspar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Label-Free Biological Reasoning Synthetic Dataset Creation via Uncertainty Filtering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:40:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:45:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic chain-of-thought (CoT) traces are widely used to train large reasoning models (LRMs), improving generalization by providing step-level supervision. Yet most approaches require ground-truth labels to seed or filter these traces - an expensive bottleneck in domains like biology where wet-lab data are scarce. We propose a label-free alternative: uncertainty-based filtering, which uses a model's own confidence - quantified through established uncertainty metrics like self-consistency and predictive perplexity - as a substitute for external labels. We sample multiple reasoning traces and retain only low-uncertainty subsets. Applied to biological perturbation prediction, a domain where wet-lab labels are especially costly, we show that the filtered subset has higher accuracy, and that supervised fine-tuning (SFT) on uncertainty-filtered data outperforms unfiltered synthetic data, narrows the gap to ground-truth training, and surpasses strong LRM baselines. Ablations show that per-class filtering corrects for class-specific uncertainty scales and that hybrid uncertainty metrics yield higher-quality datasets. Our results suggest that model-internal confidence is a powerful signal for efficient reasoning dataset creation, enabling LRMs in domains where supervision is expensive."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05871","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05865","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05865","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05865","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zifan","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Zifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongzhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aradhana","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Aradhana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongbo","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Hongbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunli","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qunli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angelo","familyName":"Cangelosi","name":"Cangelosi, Angelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Baraldi","name":"Baraldi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Safety Challenge of World Models for Embodied AI Agents: A Review"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:35:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:44:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid progress in embodied artificial intelligence has highlighted the necessity for more advanced and integrated models that can perceive, interpret, and predict environmental dynamics. In this context, World Models (WMs) have been introduced to provide embodied agents with the abilities to anticipate future environmental states and fill in knowledge gaps, thereby enhancing agents' ability to plan and execute actions. However, when dealing with embodied agents it is fundamental to ensure that predictions are safe for both the agent and the environment. In this article, we conduct a comprehensive literature review of World Models in the domains of autonomous driving and robotics, with a specific focus on the safety implications of scene and control generation tasks. Our review is complemented by an empirical analysis, wherein we collect and examine predictions from state-of-the-art models, identify and categorize common faults (herein referred to as pathologies), and provide a quantitative evaluation of the results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05865","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05827","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05827","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05827","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuanghao","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shuanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanqi","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Wanqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuedi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuedi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Badong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Badong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VCoT-Grasp: Grasp Foundation Models with Visual Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Language-driven Grasp Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:50:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic grasping is one of the most fundamental tasks in robotic manipulation, and grasp detection/generation has long been the subject of extensive research. Recently, language-driven grasp generation has emerged as a promising direction due to its practical interaction capabilities. However, most existing approaches either lack sufficient reasoning and generalization capabilities or depend on complex modular pipelines. Moreover, current grasp foundation models tend to overemphasize dialog and object semantics, resulting in inferior performance and restriction to single-object grasping. To maintain strong reasoning ability and generalization in cluttered environments, we propose VCoT-Grasp, an end-to-end grasp foundation model that incorporates visual chain-of-thought reasoning to enhance visual understanding for grasp generation. VCoT-Grasp adopts a multi-turn processing paradigm that dynamically focuses on visual inputs while providing interpretable reasoning traces. For training, we refine and introduce a large-scale dataset, VCoT-GraspSet, comprising 167K synthetic images with over 1.36M grasps, as well as 400+ real-world images with more than 1.2K grasps, annotated with intermediate bounding boxes. Extensive experiments on both VCoT-GraspSet and real robot demonstrate that our method significantly improves grasp success rates and generalizes effectively to unseen objects, backgrounds, and distractors. More details can be found at https://zhanghr2001.github.io/VCoT-Grasp.github.io."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05827","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:55Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:57:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05808","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05808","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05808","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghav","familyName":"Bongole","name":"Bongole, Raghav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirreza","familyName":"Zamani","name":"Zamani, Amirreza","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tobias J.","familyName":"Oechtering","name":"Oechtering, Tobias J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mikael","familyName":"Skoglund","name":"Skoglund, Mikael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Risk level dependent Minimax Quantile lower bounds for Interactive Statistical Decision Making"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:25:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:41:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Minimax risk and regret focus on expectation, missing rare failures critical in safety-critical bandits and reinforcement learning. Minimax quantiles capture these tails. Three strands of prior work motivate this study: minimax-quantile bounds restricted to non-interactive estimation; unified interactive analyses that focus on expected risk rather than risk level specific quantile bounds; and high-probability bandit bounds that still lack a quantile-specific toolkit for general interactive protocols. To close this gap, within the interactive statistical decision making framework, we develop high-probability Fano and Le Cam tools and derive risk level explicit minimax-quantile bounds, including a quantile-to-expectation conversion and a tight link between strict and lower minimax quantiles. Instantiating these results for the two-armed Gaussian bandit immediately recovers optimal-rate bounds."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05808","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:57:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05785","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05785","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05785","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Waldhausen","name":"Waldhausen, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Griffin","name":"Griffin, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Binary Option Market Manipulation by Influencing Belief Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Physics and Society (physics.soc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:58:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:40:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using techniques from information geometry, we construct a semi-Hamiltonian system modelling trader beliefs in a binary asset market and study the impact of inequality or asymmetry in beliefs, information, and power on price dynamics. We show that in a market with no inequality and $N$ completely symmetric traders, the resulting dynamics evolve on a $2N + 1$ dimensional manifold consisting of a $2N-2$ dimensional centre manifold, a $2$ dimensional stable manifold and a $1$ dimensional slow manifold. Introducing asymmetry into the traders has the potential to decrease the dimension of the centre manifold, which we prove using a parameter analysis. Using the belief model, we also study the impact of inter-agent communication, exogenous information and asymmetric purchasing power on price dynamics, showing that market bubbles can emerge when powerful traders produce outsize influence in the market, thus impacting other traders' beliefs as well as the price. This process is exacerbated when back-channel communication is permitted. The impact of areas of high curvature in belief space is also discussed."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05785","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:56:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05738","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05738","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05738","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritesh","familyName":"Chandra","name":"Chandra, Ritesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonali","familyName":"Agarwal","name":"Agarwal, Sonali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Navjot","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Navjot","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sadhana","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Sadhana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Review of Ontology-Driven Big Data Analytics in Healthcare: Challenges, Tools, and Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:59:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:38:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Exponential growth in heterogeneous healthcare data arising from electronic health records (EHRs), medical imaging, wearable sensors, and biomedical research has accelerated the adoption of data lakes and centralized architectures capable of handling the Volume, Variety, and Velocity of Big Data for advanced analytics. However, without effective governance, these repositories risk devolving into disorganized data swamps. Ontology-driven semantic data management offers a robust solution by linking metadata to healthcare knowledge graphs, thereby enhancing semantic interoperability, improving data discoverability, and enabling expressive, domain-aware access. This review adopts a systematic research strategy, formulating key research questions and conducting a structured literature search across major academic databases, with selected studies analyzed and classified into six categories of ontology-driven healthcare analytics: (i) ontology-driven integration frameworks, (ii) semantic modeling for metadata enrichment, (iii) ontology-based data access (OBDA), (iv) basic semantic data management, (v) ontology-based reasoning for decision support, and (vi) semantic annotation for unstructured data. We further examine the integration of ontology technologies with Big Data frameworks such as Hadoop, Spark, Kafka, and so on, highlighting their combined potential to deliver scalable and intelligent healthcare analytics. For each category, recent techniques, representative case studies, technical and organizational challenges, and emerging trends such as artificial intelligence, machine learning, the Internet of Things (IoT), and real-time analytics are reviewed to guide the development of sustainable, interoperable, and high-performance healthcare data ecosystems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05738","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:55:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05733","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05733","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05733","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijun","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Zijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuang","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Shuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinsong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jinsong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Syn-Diag: An LLM-based Synergistic Framework for Generalizable Few-shot Fault Diagnosis on the Edge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T09:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:37:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Industrial fault diagnosis faces the dual challenges of data scarcity and the difficulty of deploying large AI models in resource-constrained environments. This paper introduces Syn-Diag, a novel cloud-edge synergistic framework that leverages Large Language Models to overcome these limitations in few-shot fault diagnosis. Syn-Diag is built on a three-tiered mechanism: 1) Visual-Semantic Synergy, which aligns signal features with the LLM's semantic space through cross-modal pre-training; 2) Content-Aware Reasoning, which dynamically constructs contextual prompts to enhance diagnostic accuracy with limited samples; and 3) Cloud-Edge Synergy, which uses knowledge distillation to create a lightweight, efficient edge model capable of online updates via a shared decision space. Extensive experiments on six datasets covering different CWRU and SEU working conditions show that Syn-Diag significantly outperforms existing methods, especially in 1-shot and cross-condition scenarios. The edge model achieves performance comparable to the cloud version while reducing model size by 83% and latency by 50%, offering a practical, robust, and deployable paradigm for modern intelligent diagnostics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05733","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:55:44Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:55:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05692","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05692","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05692","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaping","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Jiaping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mir","familyName":"Feroskhan","name":"Feroskhan, Mir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Oracle-Guided Masked Contrastive Reinforcement Learning for Visuomotor Policies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:49:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A prevailing approach for learning visuomotor policies is to employ reinforcement learning to map high-dimensional visual observations directly to action commands. However, the combination of high-dimensional visual inputs and agile maneuver outputs leads to long-standing challenges, including low sample efficiency and significant sim-to-real gaps. To address these issues, we propose Oracle-Guided Masked Contrastive Reinforcement Learning (OMC-RL), a novel framework designed to improve the sample efficiency and asymptotic performance of visuomotor policy learning. OMC-RL explicitly decouples the learning process into two stages: an upstream representation learning stage and a downstream policy learning stage. In the upstream stage, a masked Transformer module is trained with temporal modeling and contrastive learning to extract temporally-aware and task-relevant representations from sequential visual inputs. After training, the learned encoder is frozen and used to extract visual representations from consecutive frames, while the Transformer module is discarded. In the downstream stage, an oracle teacher policy with privileged access to global state information supervises the agent during early training to provide informative guidance and accelerate early policy learning. This guidance is gradually reduced to allow independent exploration as training progresses. Extensive experiments in simulated and real-world environments demonstrate that OMC-RL achieves superior sample efficiency and asymptotic policy performance, while also improving generalization across diverse and perceptually complex scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05692","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:47Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:54:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongqi","familyName":"Leng","name":"Leng, Yongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikun","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Yikun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xikai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meizhi","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Meizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojian","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Bojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yurong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deyi","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Deyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DecEx-RAG: Boosting Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Decision and Execution Optimization via Process Supervision"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:49:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:34:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (Agentic RAG) enhances the processing capability for complex tasks through dynamic retrieval and adaptive workflows. Recent advances (e.g., Search-R1) have shown that outcome-supervised reinforcement learning demonstrate strong performance. However, this approach still suffers from inefficient exploration, sparse reward signals, and ambiguous global reward feedback. To address these challenges, we propose DecEx-RAG, which models RAG as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) incorporating decision-making and execution, while introducing an efficient pruning strategy to optimize data expansion. Through comprehensive process-level policy optimization, DecEx-RAG significantly enhances the autonomous task decomposition, dynamic retrieval, and high-quality answer generation capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Experiments show that DecEx-RAG achieves an average absolute performance improvement of $6.2\\%$ across six datasets, significantly outperforming existing baselines. Moreover, the pruning strategy improves data construction efficiency by nearly $6 \\times$, providing an efficient solution for process-supervised RAG training. The code is available at https://github.com/sdsxdxl/DecEx-RAG."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:45Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:54:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05663","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05663","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05663","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Senthamizhan","name":"Senthamizhan, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"R.","familyName":"Gopal","name":"Gopal, R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. K.","familyName":"Chandrasekar","name":"Chandrasekar, V. K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Swarmalators with frequency-weighted interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:18:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:32:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the role of frequency-weighted interactions in a solvable model of one-dimensional (1D) swarmalators confined to a ring, where both spatial and phase couplings are scaled by the heterogeneous natural frequencies of individual agents. Our analysis identifies three distinct collective states: the asynchronous state , the phase-wave state , and the bistrip mixed state characterized by antipodal clusters that are internally split into frequency-dependent sub-strips. We further establish that the onset of abrupt transitions are driven by heterogeneous coupling. Using a self-consistency analysis, we precisely determine the conditions for dynamical transitions among the identified states, thereby extending the theoretical understanding of swarmalator dynamics under heterogeneous interaction rules, which are in good agreement with the numerical simulation results."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 7 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05663","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:54:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:54:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:54:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiping","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyu","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shiyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Buyuan","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Buyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yipei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yipei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxuan","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yaxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiqing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang Hao","familyName":"Cheong","name":"Cheong, Kang Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EduVerse: A User-Defined Multi-Agent Simulation Space for Education Scenario"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:58:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:31:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reproducing cognitive development, group interaction, and long-term evolution in virtual classrooms remains a core challenge for educational AI, as real classrooms integrate open-ended cognition, dynamic social interaction, affective factors, and multi-session development rarely captured together. Existing approaches mostly focus on short-term or single-agent settings, limiting systematic study of classroom complexity and cross-task reuse. We present EduVerse, the first user-defined multi-agent simulation space that supports environment, agent, and session customization. A distinctive human-in-the-loop interface further allows real users to join the space. Built on a layered CIE (Cognition-Interaction-Evolution) architecture, EduVerse ensures individual consistency, authentic interaction, and longitudinal adaptation in cognition, emotion, and behavior-reproducing realistic classroom dynamics with seamless human-agent integration. We validate EduVerse in middle-school Chinese classes across three text genres, environments, and multiple sessions. Results show: (1) Instructional alignment: simulated IRF rates (0.28-0.64) closely match real classrooms (0.37-0.49), indicating pedagogical realism; (2) Group interaction and role differentiation: network density (0.27-0.40) with about one-third of peer links realized, while human-agent tasks indicate a balance between individual variability and instructional stability; (3) Cross-session evolution: the positive transition rate R+ increase by 11.7% on average, capturing longitudinal shifts in behavior, emotion, and cognition and revealing structured learning trajectories. Overall, EduVerse balances realism, reproducibility, and interpretability, providing a scalable platform for educational AI. The system will be open-sourced to foster cross-disciplinary research."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint, Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:48Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:53:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuchen","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yuchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengnan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shengnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikui","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Shikui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongyan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xiongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Danshi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Danshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generative AI-Driven Hierarchical Multi-Agent Framework for Zero-Touch Optical Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:12:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:28:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid development of Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) has catalyzed a transformative technological revolution across all walks of life. As the backbone of wideband communication, optical networks are expecting high-level autonomous operation and zero-touch management to accommodate their expanding network scales and escalating transmission bandwidth. The integration of GenAI is deemed as the pivotal solution for realizing zero-touch optical networks. However, the lifecycle management of optical networks involves a multitude of tasks and necessitates seamless collaboration across multiple layers, which poses significant challenges to the existing single-agent GenAI systems. In this paper, we propose a GenAI-driven hierarchical multi-agent framework designed to streamline multi-task autonomous execution for zero-touch optical networks. We present the architecture, implementation, and applications of this framework. A field-deployed mesh network is utilized to demonstrate three typical scenarios throughout the lifecycle of optical network: quality of transmission estimation in the planning stage, dynamic channel adding/dropping in the operation stage, and system capacity increase in the upgrade stage. The case studies, illustrate the capabilities of multi-agent framework in multi-task allocation, coordination, execution, evaluation, and summarization. This work provides a promising approach for the future development of intelligent, efficient, and collaborative network management solutions, paving the way for more specialized and adaptive zero-touch optical networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages,6 figures, Accepted by lEEE Communications Magazine, Open call"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:53:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05621","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05621","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05621","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenchi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wenchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Decoupling Correctness from Policy: A Deterministic Causal Structure for Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:10:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:28:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In distributed multi-agent systems, correctness is often entangled with operational policies such as scheduling, batching, or routing, which makes systems brittle since performance-driven policy evolution may break integrity guarantees. This paper introduces the Deterministic Causal Structure (DCS), a formal foundation that decouples correctness from policy. We develop a minimal axiomatic theory and prove four results: existence and uniqueness, policy-agnostic invariance, observational equivalence, and axiom minimality. These results show that DCS resolves causal ambiguities that value-centric convergence models such as CRDTs cannot address, and that removing any axiom collapses determinism into ambiguity. DCS thus emerges as a boundary principle of asynchronous computation, analogous to CAP and FLP: correctness is preserved only within the expressive power of a join-semilattice. All guarantees are established by axioms and proofs, with only minimal illustrative constructions included to aid intuition. This work establishes correctness as a fixed, policy-agnostic substrate, a Correctness-as-a-Chassis paradigm, on which distributed intelligent systems can be built modularly, safely, and evolvably."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05621","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:08Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:53:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05619","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05619","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05619","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Anand","name":"Anand, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheol Jun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Cheol Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Lian","name":"Lian, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gopala","familyName":"Anumanchipalli","name":"Anumanchipalli, Gopala","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teaching Machines to Speak Using Articulatory Control"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:05:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:28:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current speech production systems predominantly rely on large transformer models that operate as black boxes, providing little interpretability or grounding in the physical mechanisms of human speech. We address this limitation by proposing a new framework: speech generation through explicit articulatory control. This reframes speech as a motor control task similar to robotic manipulation. Our approach uses reinforcement learning to train a policy that directly controls the movements of vocal tract articulators, such as the tongue, lips, and jaw, to produce syllable-level speech. Specifically, we employ the Proximal Policy Optimization algorithm to learn optimal articulatory movements based on acoustic feedback provided by our audio perceiver, Sylber. The resulting articulatory trajectories are decoded into audio using SPARC, a pre-trained articulatory-to-speech decoder. We train this framework on six target syllables, and it demonstrates successful convergence, with similarity scores between the policy-generated audio and the target syllables exceeding 0.85. Accurate human transcription of the audio for syllables such as \"please\", \"loot\", and \"cat\" demonstrates the intelligibility of this framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05619","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:53:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasod","familyName":"Ginige","name":"Ginige, Yasod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akila","familyName":"Niroshan","name":"Niroshan, Akila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sajal","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Sajal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suranga","familyName":"Seneviratne","name":"Seneviratne, Suranga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AutoPentester: An LLM Agent-based Framework for Automated Pentesting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T06:02:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Penetration testing and vulnerability assessment are essential industry practices for safeguarding computer systems. As cyber threats grow in scale and complexity, the demand for pentesting has surged, surpassing the capacity of human professionals to meet it effectively. With advances in AI, particularly Large Language Models (LLMs), there have been attempts to automate the pentesting process. However, existing tools such as PentestGPT are still semi-manual, requiring significant professional human interaction to conduct pentests. To this end, we propose a novel LLM agent-based framework, AutoPentester, which automates the pentesting process. Given a target IP, AutoPentester automatically conducts pentesting steps using common security tools in an iterative process. It can dynamically generate attack strategies based on the tool outputs from the previous iteration, mimicking the human pentester approach. We evaluate AutoPentester using Hack The Box and custom-made VMs, comparing the results with the state-of-the-art PentestGPT. Results show that AutoPentester achieves a 27.0% better subtask completion rate and 39.5% more vulnerability coverage with fewer steps. Most importantly, it requires significantly fewer human interactions and interventions compared to PentestGPT. Furthermore, we recruit a group of security industry professional volunteers for a user survey and perform a qualitative analysis to evaluate AutoPentester against industry practices and compare it with PentestGPT. On average, AutoPentester received a score of 3.93 out of 5 based on user reviews, which was 19.8% higher than PentestGPT."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE TrustCom 2025 10 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:52:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changyuan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Changyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruichen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dusit","familyName":"Niyato","name":"Niyato, Dusit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianbin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xianbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwen","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Shiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abbas","familyName":"Jamalipour","name":"Jamalipour, Abbas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Agentification to Self-Evolving Agentic AI for Wireless Networks: Concepts, Approaches, and Future Research Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:45:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-evolving agentic artificial intelligence (AI) offers a new paradigm for future wireless systems by enabling autonomous agents to continually adapt and improve without human intervention. Unlike static AI models, self-evolving agents embed an autonomous evolution cycle that updates models, tools, and workflows in response to environmental dynamics. This paper presents a comprehensive overview of self-evolving agentic AI, highlighting its layered architecture, life cycle, and key techniques, including tool intelligence, workflow optimization, self-reflection, and evolutionary learning. We further propose a multi-agent cooperative self-evolving agentic AI framework, where multiple large language models (LLMs) are assigned role-specialized prompts under the coordination of a supervisor agent. Through structured dialogue, iterative feedback, and systematic validation, the system autonomously executes the entire life cycle without human intervention. A case study on antenna evolution in low-altitude wireless networks (LAWNs) demonstrates how the framework autonomously upgrades fixed antenna optimization into movable antenna optimization. Experimental results show that the proposed self-evolving agentic AI autonomously improves beam gain and restores degraded performance by up to 52.02%, consistently surpassing the fixed baseline with little to no human intervention and validating its adaptability and robustness for next-generation wireless intelligence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:52:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05593","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05593","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05593","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeqi","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Zeqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markos","familyName":"Georgopoulos","name":"Georgopoulos, Markos","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoliang","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Xiaoliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marjan","familyName":"Ghazvininejad","name":"Ghazvininejad, Marjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Juefei-Xu","name":"Juefei-Xu, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kunpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zecheng","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zecheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijian","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abe","familyName":"Davis","name":"Davis, Abe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialiang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jialiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Chain-of-Thought Efficiency for Autoregressive Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:40:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:26:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive multimodal large language models have recently gained popularity for image generation, driven by advances in foundation models. To enhance alignment and detail, newer approaches employ chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning, expanding user inputs into elaborated prompts prior to image synthesis. However, this strategy can introduce unnecessary redundancy -- a phenomenon we call visual overthinking -- which increases computational costs and can introduce details that contradict the original prompt. In this work, we explore how to generate more concise CoT sequences for more efficient image generation. We introduce ShortCoTI, a lightweight optimization framework that encourages more concise CoT while preserving output image quality. ShortCoTI rewards more concise prompts with an adaptive function that scales according to an estimated difficulty for each task. Incorporating this reward into a reinforcement learning paradigm reduces prompt reasoning length by 54% while maintaining or slightly improving quality metrics across multiple benchmarks (T2I-CompBench, GenEval). Qualitative analysis shows that our method eliminates verbose explanations and repetitive refinements, producing reasoning prompts that are both concise and semantically rich. As a result, ShortCoTI improves computational efficiency without compromising the fidelity or visual appeal of generated images."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05593","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:52:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05564","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05564","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05564","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhyoung","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Junhyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Olivati","name":"Olivati, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mirko","familyName":"Prato","name":"Prato, Mirko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annamaria","familyName":"Petrozza","name":"Petrozza, Annamaria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Co-evaporated Formamidinium tin triiodide with suppressed p-type self-doping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:18:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Co-evaporation of formamidinium tin triiodide (FASnI3) precursors, without any additives or reducing agents, leads to the growth of a highly crystalline thin film which shows a bandgap around 1.31 eV, closely matching the theoretical value predicted from the ideal single crystal structure of FASnI3. The polycrystalline thin film presents a lower tendency of Sn2+ to Sn4+ oxidation and highly reduced tendency to self-doping, demonstrating, overall, an improved resistance to defects formation. These findings suggest solvent-free co-evaporation processes as a promising route for high quality Sn-based perovskite polycrystalline thin films."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 Pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05564","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:48Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05556","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05556","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05556","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiakai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kostis","familyName":"Kaffes","name":"Kaffes, Kostis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Systems Foundations for Agentic Exploration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Operating Systems (cs.OS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:57:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic exploration, letting LLM-powered agents branch, backtrack, and search across many execution paths, demands systems support well beyond today's pass-at-k resets. Our benchmark of six snapshot/restore mechanisms shows that generic tools such as CRIU or container commits are not fast enough even in isolated testbeds, and they crumble entirely in real deployments where agents share files, sockets, and cloud APIs with other agents and human users. In this talk, we pinpoint three open fundamental challenges: fork semantics, which concerns how branches reveal or hide tentative updates; external side-effects, where fork awareness must be added to services or their calls intercepted; and native forking, which requires cloning databases and runtimes in microseconds without bulk copying."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05556","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:37Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05553","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05553","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05553","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan Rui","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yan Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqi","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wai Lun","familyName":"Leong","name":"Leong, Wai Lun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John Guan Zhong","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, John Guan Zhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wayne Wen Huei","familyName":"Yong","name":"Yong, Wayne Wen Huei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodney Swee Huat","familyName":"Teo","name":"Teo, Rodney Swee Huat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GO-Flock: Goal-Oriented Flocking in 3D Unknown Environments with Depth Maps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:46:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:22:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Artificial Potential Field (APF) methods are widely used for reactive flocking control, but they often suffer from challenges such as deadlocks and local minima, especially in the presence of obstacles. Existing solutions to address these issues are typically passive, leading to slow and inefficient collective navigation. As a result, many APF approaches have only been validated in obstacle-free environments or simplified, pseudo 3D simulations. This paper presents GO-Flock, a hybrid flocking framework that integrates planning with reactive APF-based control. GO-Flock consists of an upstream Perception Module, which processes depth maps to extract waypoints and virtual agents for obstacle avoidance, and a downstream Collective Navigation Module, which applies a novel APF strategy to achieve effective flocking behavior in cluttered environments. We evaluate GO-Flock against passive APF-based approaches to demonstrate their respective merits, such as their flocking behavior and the ability to overcome local minima. Finally, we validate GO-Flock through obstacle-filled environment and also hardware-in-the-loop experiments where we successfully flocked a team of nine drones, six physical and three virtual, in a forest environment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05553","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05547","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05547","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05547","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eugene","familyName":"Vorobiov","name":"Vorobiov, Eugene","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ammar Jaleel","familyName":"Mahmood","name":"Mahmood, Ammar Jaleel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salim","familyName":"Rezvani","name":"Rezvani, Salim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Chhabra","name":"Chhabra, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARRC: Advanced Reasoning Robot Control - Knowledge-Driven Autonomous Manipulation Using Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T03:20:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:21:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present ARRC (Advanced Reasoning Robot Control), a practical system that connects natural-language instructions to safe local robotic control by combining Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with RGB-D perception and guarded execution on an affordable robot arm. The system indexes curated robot knowledge (movement patterns, task templates, and safety heuristics) in a vector database, retrieves task-relevant context for each instruction, and conditions a large language model (LLM) to produce JSON-structured action plans. Plans are executed on a UFactory xArm 850 fitted with a Dynamixel-driven parallel gripper and an Intel RealSense D435 camera. Perception uses AprilTag detections fused with depth to produce object-centric metric poses. Execution is enforced via software safety gates: workspace bounds, speed and force caps, timeouts, and bounded retries. We describe the architecture, knowledge design, integration choices, and a reproducible evaluation protocol for tabletop scan, approach, and pick-place tasks. Experimental results demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed approach. Our design shows that RAG-based planning can substantially improve plan validity and adaptability while keeping perception and low-level control local to the robot."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05547","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05533","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05533","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05533","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weilong","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Weilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The New Quant: A Survey of Large Language Models in Financial Prediction and Trading"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Portfolio Management (q-fin.PM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:46:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models are reshaping quantitative investing by turning unstructured financial information into evidence-grounded signals and executable decisions. This survey synthesizes research with a focus on equity return prediction and trading, consolidating insights from domain surveys and more than fifty primary studies. We propose a task-centered taxonomy that spans sentiment and event extraction, numerical and economic reasoning, multimodal understanding, retrieval-augmented generation, time series prompting, and agentic systems that coordinate tools for research, backtesting, and execution. We review empirical evidence for predictability, highlight design patterns that improve faithfulness such as retrieval first prompting and tool-verified numerics, and explain how signals feed portfolio construction under exposure, turnover, and capacity controls. We assess benchmarks and datasets for prediction and trading and outline desiderata-for time safe and economically meaningful evaluation that reports costs, latency, and capacity. We analyze challenges that matter in production, including temporal leakage, hallucination, data coverage and structure, deployment economics, interpretability, governance, and safety. The survey closes with recommendations for standardizing evaluation, building auditable pipelines, and advancing multilingual and cross-market research so that language-driven systems deliver robust and risk-controlled performance in practice."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"21 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05533","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshil","familyName":"Vejendla","name":"Vejendla, Harshil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"H1B-KV: Hybrid One-Bit Caches for Memory-Efficient Large Language Model Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:39:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive decoding in large language models (LLMs) requires caching a growing list of past key-value (KV) pairs, making long-context inference a memory-bound problem. While recent methods have explored quantizing the cache, evicting tokens, or using binary sketches for keys (e.g., Loki), these approaches often provide an incomplete solution by leaving one component (like values) uncompressed or by discarding context information. This paper introduces the Hybrid One-Bit KV Cache (H1B-KV), a comprehensive compression scheme that radically reduces memory usage without sacrificing context. H1B-KV represents each key vector using a 1-bit binary sketch, enabling hardware-friendly bitwise attention, and further compresses value vectors using 4-bit quantization. This holistic, hybrid approach allows a 7-billion parameter LLM to handle an 8k-token context with under 60 MB of cache memory - a 70x reduction. We demonstrate that after a lightweight finetuning, H1B-KV matches full-precision performance not only on perplexity benchmarks but also on complex downstream tasks like mathematical reasoning (GSM8K), multi-task understanding (MMLU), and code generation (HumanEval). Our results show H1B-KV significantly outperforms leading quantization (KIVI), token eviction (SparseLLM), and key-only sketching (Loki) methods in quality-per-byte, establishing it as a robust solution for deploying LLMs in memory-constrained environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MIT URTC 2025 Technical Paper (Oral), 5 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:59Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05520","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05520","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05520","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohe","familyName":"Bo","name":"Bo, Xiaohe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihang","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Zihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanyu","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Quanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ruiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CAM: A Constructivist View of Agentic Memory for LLM-Based Reading Comprehension"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:16:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current Large Language Models (LLMs) are confronted with overwhelming information volume when comprehending long-form documents. This challenge raises the imperative of a cohesive memory module, which can elevate vanilla LLMs into autonomous reading agents. Despite the emergence of some heuristic approaches, a systematic design principle remains absent. To fill this void, we draw inspiration from Jean Piaget's Constructivist Theory, illuminating three traits of the agentic memory -- structured schemata, flexible assimilation, and dynamic accommodation. This blueprint forges a clear path toward a more robust and efficient memory system for LLM-based reading comprehension. To this end, we develop CAM, a prototype implementation of Constructivist Agentic Memory that simultaneously embodies the structurality, flexibility, and dynamicity. At its core, CAM is endowed with an incremental overlapping clustering algorithm for structured memory development, supporting both coherent hierarchical summarization and online batch integration. During inference, CAM adaptively explores the memory structure to activate query-relevant information for contextual response, akin to the human associative process. Compared to existing approaches, our design demonstrates dual advantages in both performance and efficiency across diverse long-text reading comprehension tasks, including question answering, query-based summarization, and claim verification."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05520","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:46Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:50:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ceyhun Efe","familyName":"Kayan","name":"Kayan, Ceyhun Efe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prototype-Based Dynamic Steering for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:34:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite impressive breadth, LLMs still rely on explicit reasoning instructions or static, one-fits-all steering methods, leaving a gap for adaptive, instruction-free reasoning amplification. We present Prototype-Based Dynamic Steering (PDS), a test-time method that amplifies large language model (LLM) reasoning without adding or altering instructions. We introduce \"reasoning prototypes\" by clustering activation differences between Chain-of-Thought (CoT) and neutral prompts. At inference, an input's hidden state is projected onto these prototypes to form an instance-specific steering vector. Evaluated on GSM8K, AQuA-RAT, and BIG-Bench tasks, PDS consistently improves accuracy without fine-tuning or prompt engineering. Notably, the gains persist even when CoT is explicitly suppressed to improve cost-efficiency, indicating that the intervention strengthens latent reasoning processes rather than inducing a superficial behavioral shift. These results position dynamic, prototype-guided steering as a lightweight alternative to training-time approaches for enhancing LLM reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:50:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05485","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05485","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05485","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Filipek","name":"Filipek, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TensorBLEU: Vectorized GPU-based BLEU Score Implementation for Per-Sentence In-Training Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern natural language processing models have achieved unprecedented scale, yet the tools for their evaluation often remain a computational bottleneck, limiting the pace of research. This is particularly acute for in-training evaluation metrics, such as per-sentence reward signals in Reinforcement Learning, which must operate efficiently on batches of token IDs directly on the GPU. In this paper, we introduce TensorBLEU, a novel implementation of the BLEU metric designed from the ground up for this specific use case. Our approach is fully vectorized for GPU-accelerated, per-sentence computation within PyTorch and introduces a memory-efficient counting mechanism. By creating a compact, batch-specific dictionary of n-grams using \\texttt{torch.unique}, our method avoids the prohibitive memory costs of traditional hashing-based vectorization, making it practical for large-vocabulary models. We benchmark TensorBLEU against NLTK, the standard library for token-ID-based BLEU calculation on the CPU. Experiments show that TensorBLEU provides speedups of over 13x on consumer-grade GPUs (NVIDIA T4) and exceeding 40x on data-center-class hardware (NVIDIA A100). This performance transforms a significant bottleneck into a negligible part of the training loop. By clearly defining its role as a \"Token-ID BLEU\" for development purposes and open-sourcing our implementation, we provide a powerful tool for accelerating research in areas like RL-based model fine-tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05485","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin-Cheng","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Xin-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cuiyun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Cuiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deheng","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Deheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Vul-R2: A Reasoning LLM for Automated Vulnerability Repair"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The exponential increase in software vulnerabilities has created an urgent need for automatic vulnerability repair (AVR) solutions. Recent research has formulated AVR as a sequence generation problem and has leveraged large language models (LLMs) to address this problem. Typically, these approaches prompt or fine-tune LLMs to generate repairs for vulnerabilities directly. Although these methods show state-of-the-art performance, they face the following challenges: (1) Lack of high-quality, vulnerability-related reasoning data. Current approaches primarily rely on foundation models that mainly encode general programming knowledge. Without vulnerability-related reasoning data, they tend to fail to capture the diverse vulnerability repair patterns. (2) Hard to verify the intermediate vulnerability repair process during LLM training. Existing reinforcement learning methods often leverage intermediate execution feedback from the environment (e.g., sandbox-based execution results) to guide reinforcement learning training. In contrast, the vulnerability repair process generally lacks such intermediate, verifiable feedback, which poses additional challenges for model training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 8 figures. This paper is accepted by ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Bagarello","name":"Bagarello, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Francesco","familyName":"Gargano","name":"Gargano, Francesco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Polina","familyName":"Khrennikova","name":"Khrennikova, Polina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Classical Rationality to Contextual Reasoning: Quantum Logic as a New Frontier for Human-Centric AI in Finance"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:31:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider state of the art applications of artificial intelligence (AI) in modelling human financial expectations and explore the potential of quantum logic to drive future advancements in this field. This analysis highlights the application of machine learning techniques, including reinforcement learning and deep neural networks, in financial statement analysis, algorithmic trading, portfolio management, and robo-advisory services. We further discuss the emergence and progress of quantum machine learning (QML) and advocate for broader exploration of the advantages provided by quantum-inspired neural networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"19 pages, 5 figures, preprint version. Forthcoming in: Journal of Quantum Economics and Finance"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:45Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05474","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05474","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05474","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marios","familyName":"Mertzanidis","name":"Mertzanidis, Marios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Athina","familyName":"Terzoglou","name":"Terzoglou, Athina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hallucinating Flows for Optimal Mechanisms"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:29:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:16:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Myerson's seminal characterization of the revenue-optimal auction for a single item \\cite{myerson1981optimal} remains a cornerstone of mechanism design. However, generalizing this framework to multi-item settings has proven exceptionally challenging. Even under restrictive assumptions, closed-form characterizations of optimal mechanisms are rare and are largely confined to the single-agent case \\cite{pavlov2011optimal,hart2017approximate, daskalakis2018transport, GIANNAKOPOULOS2018432}, departing from the two-item setting only when prior distributions are uniformly distributed \\cite{manelli2006bundling, daskalakis2017strong,giannakopoulos2018sjm}. In this work, we build upon the bi-valued setting introduced by Yao \\cite{YAO_BIC_DSIC}, where each item's value has support 2 and lies in $\\{a, b\\}$. Yao's result provides the only known closed-form optimal mechanism for multiple agents. We extend this line of work along three natural axes, establishing the first closed-form optimal mechanisms in each of the following settings: (i) $n$ i.i.d. agents and $m$ i.i.d. items (ii) $n$ non-i.i.d. agents and two i.i.d. items and (iii) $n$ i.i.d. agents and two non-i.i.d. items. Our results lie at the limit of what is considered possible, since even with a single agent and m bi-valued non-i.i.d. items, finding the optimal mechanism is $\\#P$-Hard \\cite{daskalakis2014complexity, xi2018soda}. We finally generalize the discrete analog of a result from~\\cite{daskalakis2017strong}, showing that for a single agent with $m$ items drawn from arbitrary (non-identical) discrete distributions, grand bundling is optimal when all item values are sufficiently large. We further show that for any continuous product distribution, grand bundling achieves $\\mathrm{OPT} - ε$ revenue for large enough values."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05474","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:43Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05460","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05460","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05460","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jabari","familyName":"Hastings","name":"Hastings, Jabari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasanna","familyName":"Ramakrishnan","name":"Ramakrishnan, Prasanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fair metric distortion for matching with preferences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:47:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the matching problem in the metric distortion framework. There are $n$ agents and $n$ items occupying points in a shared metric space, and the goal is to design a matching mechanism that outputs a low-cost matching between the agents and items, using only agents' ordinal rankings of the candidates by distance. A mechanism has distortion $α$ if it always outputs a matching whose cost is within a factor of $α$ of the optimum, in every instance regardless of the metric space.\n Typically, the cost of a matching is measured in terms of the total distance between matched agents and items, but this measure can incentivize unfair outcomes where a handful of agents bear the brunt of the cost. With this in mind, we consider how the metric distortion problem changes when the cost is instead measured in terms of the maximum cost of any agent. We show that while these two notions of distortion can in general differ by a factor of $n$, the distortion of a variant of the state-of-the-art mechanism, RepMatch, actually improves from $O(n^2)$ under the sum objective to $O(n^{1.58})$ under the max objective. We also show that for any fairness objective defined by a monotone symmetric norm, this algorithm guarantees distortion $O(n^2)$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear at WINE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05460","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:24Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05458","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05458","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05458","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akhil","familyName":"Deo","name":"Deo, Akhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kate","familyName":"Sanders","name":"Sanders, Kate","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Van Durme","name":"Van Durme, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SocialNLI: A Dialogue-Centric Social Inference Dataset"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:42:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Making theory-of-mind inferences from human dialogue is a strong indicator of a model's underlying social abilities, which are fundamental for adept AI assistants. However, large language and reasoning models struggle to understand sophisticated social phenomena in transcript data, such as sarcasm and irony. To assess the weaknesses of current models and to identify their solutions, we introduce SocialNLI (SoNLI) -- the first social dialogue inference dataset. SoNLI consists of a collection of dialogue transcripts hand-picked to center complex social nuances like irony and sarcasm, paired with inferences, corresponding likelihood scores, and human-written explanations. We explore social inference analysis as a facet of theory-of-mind, and evaluate LLM and reasoning model theory-of-mind ability through multi-step counterfactual reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05458","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05451","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05451","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05451","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fadi Al","familyName":"Machot","name":"Machot, Fadi Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fidaa Al","familyName":"Machot","name":"Machot, Fidaa Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NASP-T: A Fuzzy Neuro-Symbolic Transformer for Logic-Constrained Aviation Safety Report Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:33:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep transformer models excel at multi-label text classification but often violate domain logic that experts consider essential, an issue of particular concern in safety-critical applications. We propose a hybrid neuro-symbolic framework that integrates Answer Set Programming (ASP) with transformer-based learning on the Aviation Safety Reporting System (ASRS) corpus. Domain knowledge is formalized as weighted ASP rules and validated using the Clingo solver. These rules are incorporated in two complementary ways: (i) as rule-based data augmentation, generating logically consistent synthetic samples that improve label diversity and coverage; and (ii) as a fuzzy-logic regularizer, enforcing rule satisfaction in a differentiable form during fine-tuning. This design preserves the interpretability of symbolic reasoning while leveraging the scalability of deep neural architectures. We further tune per-class thresholds and report both standard classification metrics and logic-consistency rates. Compared to a strong Binary Cross-Entropy (BCE) baseline, our approach improves micro- and macro-F1 scores and achieves up to an 86% reduction in rule violations on the ASRS test set. To the best of our knowledge, this constitutes the first large-scale neuro-symbolic application to ASRS reports that unifies ASP-based reasoning, rule-driven augmentation, and differentiable transformer training for trustworthy, safety-critical NLP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05451","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05446","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05446","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05446","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runlin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Runlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chixiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elynn","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Elynn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prior-Aligned Meta-RL: Thompson Sampling with Learned Priors and Guarantees in Finite-Horizon MDPs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:20:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study meta-reinforcement learning in finite-horizon MDPs where related tasks share similar structures in their optimal action-value functions. Specifically, we posit a linear representation $Q^*_h(s,a)=Φ_h(s,a)\\,θ^{(k)}_h$ and place a Gaussian meta-prior $ \\mathcal{N}(θ^*_h,Σ^*_h)$ over the task-specific parameters $θ^{(k)}_h$. Building on randomized value functions, we propose two Thompson-style algorithms: (i) MTSRL, which learns only the prior mean and performs posterior sampling with the learned mean and known covariance; and (ii) $\\text{MTSRL}^{+}$, which additionally estimates the covariance and employs prior widening to control finite-sample estimation error. Further, we develop a prior-alignment technique that couples the posterior under the learned prior with a meta-oracle that knows the true prior, yielding meta-regret guarantees: we match prior-independent Thompson sampling in the small-task regime and strictly improve with more tasks once the prior is learned. Concretely, for known covariance we obtain $\\tilde{O}(H^{4}S^{3/2}\\sqrt{ANK})$ meta-regret, and with learned covariance $\\tilde{O}(H^{4}S^{3/2}\\sqrt{AN^3K})$; both recover a better behavior than prior-independent after $K \\gtrsim \\tilde{O}(H^2)$ and $K \\gtrsim \\tilde{O}(N^2H^2)$, respectively. Simulations on a stateful recommendation environment (with feature and prior misspecification) show that after brief exploration, MTSRL/MTSRL\\(^+\\) track the meta-oracle and substantially outperform prior-independent RL and bandit-only meta-baselines. Our results give the first meta-regret guarantees for Thompson-style RL with learned Q-priors, and provide practical recipes (warm-start via RLSVI, OLS aggregation, covariance widening) for experiment-rich settings."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05446","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05445","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05445","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05445","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zheyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengqing","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Zhengqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zehong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keerthiram","familyName":"Murugesan","name":"Murugesan, Keerthiram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Galassi","name":"Galassi, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuxu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chuxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yanfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentRouter: A Knowledge-Graph-Guided LLM Router for Collaborative Multi-Agent Question Answering"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:20:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) and agent-based frameworks have advanced rapidly, enabling diverse applications. Yet, with the proliferation of models and agentic strategies, practitioners face substantial uncertainty in selecting the best configuration for a downstream task. Prior studies show that different agents and backbones exhibit complementary strengths, and that larger models are not always superior, underscoring the need for adaptive routing mechanisms. Existing approaches to agent routing, however, often emphasize cost efficiency while overlooking the fine-grained contextual and relational structure inherent in QA tasks. In this paper, we propose tAgentRouter, a framework that formulates multi-agent QA as a knowledge-graph-guided routing problem supervised by empirical performance signals. Specifically, we convert QA instance into a knowledge graph that jointly encodes queries, contextual entities, and agents, and then train a heterogeneous graph neural network (GNN) to propagate information across node types and produce task-aware routing distributions over agents. By leveraging soft supervision and weighted aggregation of agent outputs, AgentRouter learns principled collaboration schemes that capture the complementary strengths of diverse agents. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our framework consistently outperforms single-agent and ensemble baselines, while generalizing across benchmarks and LLM backbones. These results highlight the effectiveness and robustness of graph-supervised multi-agent routing for question answering."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05445","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05442","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05442","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05442","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zizhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingcheng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Dingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vaishakh","familyName":"Keshava","name":"Keshava, Vaishakh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phillip","familyName":"Wallis","name":"Wallis, Phillip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ananth","familyName":"Balashankar","name":"Balashankar, Ananth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Stone","name":"Stone, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Rutishauser","name":"Rutishauser, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Reinforcement Learning for Large Language Model Agent Safety"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:09:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM) agents can leverage tools such as Google Search to complete complex tasks. However, this tool usage introduces the risk of indirect prompt injections, where malicious instructions hidden in tool outputs can manipulate the agent, posing security risks like data leakage. Current defense strategies typically rely on fine-tuning LLM agents on datasets of known attacks. However, the generation of these datasets relies on manually crafted attack patterns, which limits their diversity and leaves agents vulnerable to novel prompt injections. To address this limitation, we propose Adversarial Reinforcement Learning for Agent Safety (ARLAS), a novel framework that leverages adversarial reinforcement learning (RL) by formulating the problem as a two-player zero-sum game. ARLAS co-trains two LLMs: an attacker that learns to autonomously generate diverse prompt injections and an agent that learns to defend against them while completing its assigned tasks. To ensure robustness against a wide range of attacks and to prevent cyclic learning, we employ a population-based learning framework that trains the agent to defend against all previous attacker checkpoints. Evaluated on BrowserGym and AgentDojo, agents fine-tuned with ARLAS achieve a significantly lower attack success rate than the original model while also improving their task success rate. Our analysis further confirms that the adversarial process generates a diverse and challenging set of attacks, leading to a more robust agent compared to the base model."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05442","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:00Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05441","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05441","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05441","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiannis","familyName":"Charalambous","name":"Charalambous, Yiannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claudionor N.","familyName":"Coelho","name":"Coelho, Claudionor N.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"Lamb","name":"Lamb, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucas C.","familyName":"Cordeiro","name":"Cordeiro, Lucas C.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UnitTenX: Generating Tests for Legacy Packages with AI Agents Powered by Formal Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:08:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper introduces UnitTenX, a state-of-the-art open-source AI multi-agent system designed to generate unit tests for legacy code, enhancing test coverage and critical value testing. UnitTenX leverages a combination of AI agents, formal methods, and Large Language Models (LLMs) to automate test generation, addressing the challenges posed by complex and legacy codebases. Despite the limitations of LLMs in bug detection, UnitTenX offers a robust framework for improving software reliability and maintainability. Our results demonstrate the effectiveness of this approach in generating high-quality tests and identifying potential issues. Additionally, our approach enhances the readability and documentation of legacy code."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05441","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:48:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05434","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05434","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05434","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohith Reddy","familyName":"Gangam","name":"Gangam, Rohith Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shayan","familyName":"Taherijam","name":"Taherijam, Shayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay V.","familyName":"Vazirani","name":"Vazirani, Vijay V.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fair Rent Division: New Budget and Rent Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:53:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study the classical rent division problem, where $n$ agents must allocate $n$ indivisible rooms and split a fixed total rent $R$. The goal is to compute an envy-free (EF) allocation, where no agent prefers another agent's room and rent to their own. This problem has been extensively studied under standard assumptions, where efficient algorithms for computing EF allocations are known.\n We extend this framework by introducing two practically motivated constraints: (i) lower and upper bounds on room rents, and (ii) room-specific budget for agents. We develop efficient combinatorial algorithms that either compute a feasible EF allocation or certify infeasibility.\n We further design algorithms to optimize over EF allocations using natural fairness objectives such as maximin utility, leximin utility, and minimum utility spread. Our approach unifies both constraint types within a single algorithmic framework, advancing the applicability of fair division methods in real-world platforms such as Spliddit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05434","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05429","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05429","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05429","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simina","familyName":"Brânzei","name":"Brânzei, Simina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Computing Envy-Free up to Any Good (EFX) Allocations via Local Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:48:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:14:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a simple local search algorithm for computing EFX (envy-free up to any good) allocations of $m$ indivisible goods among $n$ agents with additive valuations. EFX is a compelling fairness notion, and whether such allocations always exist remains a major open question in fair division.\n Our algorithm employs simulated annealing with the total number of EFX violations as an objective function together with a single-transfer neighborhood structure to move through the space of allocations. It found an EFX allocation in all the instances tested, which included thousands of randomly generated inputs, and scaled to settings with hundreds of agents and/or thousands of items. The algorithm's simplicity, along with its strong empirical performance makes it a simple benchmark for evaluating future approaches.\n On the theoretical side, we provide a potential function for identical additive valuations, which ensures that any strict-descent procedure under the single-transfer neighborhood ends at an EFX allocation. This represents an alternative proof of existence for identical valuations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 5 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05429","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:42Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:48:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05414","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05414","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05414","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziheng","familyName":"Geng","name":"Geng, Ziheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiachen","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiachen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haifeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghui","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Minghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Lightweight Large Language Model-Based Multi-Agent System for 2D Frame Structural Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T22:12:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:12:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently been used to empower autonomous agents in engineering, significantly improving automation and efficiency in labor-intensive workflows. However, their potential remains underexplored in structural engineering, particularly for finite element modeling tasks requiring geometric modeling, complex reasoning, and domain knowledge. To bridge this gap, this paper develops a LLM-based multi-agent system to automate finite element modeling of 2D frames. The system decomposes structural analysis into subtasks, each managed by a specialized agent powered by the lightweight Llama-3.3 70B Instruct model. The workflow begins with a Problem Analysis Agent, which extracts geometry, boundary, and material parameters from the user input. Next, a Geometry Agent incrementally derives node coordinates and element connectivity by applying expert-defined rules. These structured outputs are converted into executable OpenSeesPy code by a Translation Agent and refined by a Model Validation Agent through consistency checks. Then, a Load Agent applies load conditions into the assembled structural model. Experimental evaluations on 20 benchmark problems demonstrate that the system achieves accuracy over 80% in most cases across 10 repeated trials, outperforming Gemini-2.5 Pro and ChatGPT-4o models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05414","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:48:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05402","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05402","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05402","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmad","familyName":"Alsheikh","name":"Alsheikh, Ahmad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andreas","familyName":"Fischer","name":"Fischer, Andreas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Teacher-Student Guided Inverse Modeling for Steel Final Hardness Estimation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.6.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:50:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:12:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting the final hardness of steel after heat treatment is a challenging regression task due to the many-to-one nature of the process -- different combinations of input parameters (such as temperature, duration, and chemical composition) can result in the same hardness value. This ambiguity makes the inverse problem, estimating input parameters from a desired hardness, particularly difficult. In this work, we propose a novel solution using a Teacher-Student learning framework. First, a forward model (Teacher) is trained to predict final hardness from 13 metallurgical input features. Then, a backward model (Student) is trained to infer plausible input configurations from a target hardness value. The Student is optimized by leveraging feedback from the Teacher in an iterative, supervised loop. We evaluate our method on a publicly available tempered steel dataset and compare it against baseline regression and reinforcement learning models. Results show that our Teacher-Student framework not only achieves higher inverse prediction accuracy but also requires significantly less computational time, demonstrating its effectiveness and efficiency for inverse process modeling in materials science."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Workshop paper, AIP2025: Second Workshop on AI in Production (2025). Licensed under CC BY 4.0"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05402","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:48:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:48:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:48:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05365","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05365","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05365","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Jack"],"givenName":"Irtaza Sajid","familyName":"Qureshi","name":"Qureshi, Irtaza Sajid","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Jack"],"givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Ming","name":"Ming, Zhen","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Jiang","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test Case Generation from Bug Reports via Large Language Models: A Cognitive Layered Evaluation Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:47:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly applied to automated software testing, yet their ability to generalize beyond memorized patterns and reason about natural language bug reports remains unclear. We present a systematic evaluation of LLM reasoning in test case generation, structured around the cognitive layers of Bloom's taxonomy: \\textit{Remember}, \\textit{Understand}, \\textit{Apply}, \\textit{Analyze}, \\textit{Evaluate}, and \\textit{Create}, which progressively assess higher levels of cognitive and reasoning capabilities. Building on the LIBRO framework, we evaluate StarCoder and GPT-4o on Defects4J, GHRB, and mutated variants that introduce linguistic and semantic challenges. Our findings show that both models largely reproduce prior results with minor deviations (\\textit{Remember}), exhibit partial robustness to linguistic rephrasings and translations while uncovering unique reproducible bugs (\\textit{Understand}), but suffer severe performance drops exceeding 60\\% under identifier mutations (\\textit{Apply}). Conversely, providing near-identical few-shot examples in an open-book setting improves success rates by up to three times, and component-level analysis reveals that structured technical elements, such as test code and method names, are far more impactful than narrative descriptions for successful test generation (\\textit{Analyze}). These insights illuminate the cognitive processes underlying LLM-generated tests, suggest concrete directions for improving performance, and establish a robust and realistic evaluation paradigm for this task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05365","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:47:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pegah","familyName":"Alipoormolabashi","name":"Alipoormolabashi, Pegah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihu","familyName":"Mun","name":"Mun, Jihu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gourab","familyName":"Dey","name":"Dey, Gourab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Soni","name":"Soni, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niranjan","familyName":"Balasubramanian","name":"Balasubramanian, Niranjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Owen","familyName":"Rambow","name":"Rambow, Owen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"H.","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Residualized Similarity for Faithfully Explainable Authorship Verification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:41:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Responsible use of Authorship Verification (AV) systems not only requires high accuracy but also interpretable solutions. More importantly, for systems to be used to make decisions with real-world consequences requires the model's prediction to be explainable using interpretable features that can be traced to the original texts. Neural methods achieve high accuracies, but their representations lack direct interpretability. Furthermore, LLM predictions cannot be explained faithfully -- if there is an explanation given for a prediction, it doesn't represent the reasoning process behind the model's prediction. In this paper, we introduce Residualized Similarity (RS), a novel method that supplements systems using interpretable features with a neural network to improve their performance while maintaining interpretability. Authorship verification is fundamentally a similarity task, where the goal is to measure how alike two documents are. The key idea is to use the neural network to predict a similarity residual, i.e. the error in the similarity predicted by the interpretable system. Our evaluation across four datasets shows that not only can we match the performance of state-of-the-art authorship verification models, but we can show how and to what degree the final prediction is faithful and interpretable."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:47:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05335","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05335","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05335","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oskar","familyName":"Wysocki","name":"Wysocki, Oskar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magdalena","familyName":"Wysocka","name":"Wysocka, Magdalena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mauricio","familyName":"Jacobo","name":"Jacobo, Mauricio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harriet","familyName":"Unsworth","name":"Unsworth, Harriet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Freitas","name":"Freitas, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Biomedical reasoning in action: Multi-agent System for Auditable Biomedical Evidence Synthesis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:57:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:08:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present M-Reason, a demonstration system for transparent, agent-based reasoning and evidence integration in the biomedical domain, with a focus on cancer research. M-Reason leverages recent advances in large language models (LLMs) and modular agent orchestration to automate evidence retrieval, appraisal, and synthesis across diverse biomedical data sources. Each agent specializes in a specific evidence stream, enabling parallel processing and fine-grained analysis. The system emphasizes explainability, structured reporting, and user auditability, providing complete traceability from source evidence to final conclusions. We discuss critical tradeoffs between agent specialization, system complexity, and resource usage, as well as the integration of deterministic code for validation. An open, interactive user interface allows researchers to directly observe, explore and evaluate the multi-agent workflow. Our evaluation demonstrates substantial gains in efficiency and output consistency, highlighting M-Reason's potential as both a practical tool for evidence synthesis and a testbed for robust multi-agent LLM systems in scientific research, available at https://m-reason.digitalecmt.com."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05335","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:46:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05327","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05327","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05327","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zahin","familyName":"Ibnat","name":"Ibnat, Zahin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paul E.","familyName":"Calzada","name":"Calzada, Paul E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rasin Mohammed","familyName":"Ihtemam","name":"Ihtemam, Rasin Mohammed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sujan Kumar","familyName":"Saha","name":"Saha, Sujan Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingbo","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farimah","familyName":"Farahmandi","name":"Farahmandi, Farimah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mark","familyName":"Tehranipoor","name":"Tehranipoor, Mark","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepV: A Model-Agnostic Retrieval-Augmented Framework for Verilog Code Generation with a High-Quality Knowledge Base"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T19:47:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) continue to be integrated into modern technology, there has been an increased push towards code generation applications, which also naturally extends to hardware design automation. LLM-based solutions for register transfer level (RTL) code generation for intellectual property (IP) designs have grown, especially with fine-tuned LLMs, prompt engineering, and agentic approaches becoming popular in literature. However, a gap has been exposed in these techniques, as they fail to integrate novel IPs into the model's knowledge base, subsequently resulting in poorly generated code. Additionally, as general-purpose LLMs continue to improve, fine-tuned methods on older models will not be able to compete to produce more accurate and efficient designs. Although some retrieval augmented generation (RAG) techniques exist to mitigate challenges presented in fine-tuning approaches, works tend to leverage low-quality codebases, incorporate computationally expensive fine-tuning in the frameworks, or do not use RAG directly in the RTL generation step. In this work, we introduce DeepV: a model-agnostic RAG framework to generate RTL designs by enhancing context through a large, high-quality dataset without any RTL-specific training. Our framework benefits the latest commercial LLM, OpenAI's GPT-5, with a near 17% increase in performance on the VerilogEval benchmark. We host DeepV for use by the community in a Hugging Face (HF) Space: https://huggingface.co/spaces/FICS-LLM/DeepV."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05327","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:46:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:46:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhicheng","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Zhicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adjusting the Output of Decision Transformer with Action Gradient"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:54:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:06:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Decision Transformer (DT), which integrates reinforcement learning (RL) with the transformer model, introduces a novel approach to offline RL. Unlike classical algorithms that take maximizing cumulative discounted rewards as objective, DT instead maximizes the likelihood of actions. This paradigm shift, however, presents two key challenges: stitching trajectories and extrapolation of action. Existing methods, such as substituting specific tokens with predictive values and integrating the Policy Gradient (PG) method, address these challenges individually but fail to improve performance stably when combined due to inherent instability. To address this, we propose Action Gradient (AG), an innovative methodology that directly adjusts actions to fulfill a function analogous to that of PG, while also facilitating efficient integration with token prediction techniques. AG utilizes the gradient of the Q-value with respect to the action to optimize the action. The empirical results demonstrate that our method can significantly enhance the performance of DT-based algorithms, with some results achieving state-of-the-art levels."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05283","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05283","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05283","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Radha","familyName":"Gulhane","name":"Gulhane, Radha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sathish Reddy","familyName":"Indurthi","name":"Indurthi, Sathish Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Monolithic Rewards: A Hybrid and Multi-Aspect Reward Optimization for MLLM Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:53:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning multimodal large language models (MLLMs) with human preferences often relies on single-signal, model-based reward methods. Such monolithic rewards often lack confidence calibration across domain-specific tasks, fail to capture diverse aspects of human preferences, and require extensive data annotation and reward model training. In this work, we propose a hybrid reward modeling framework that integrates complementary reward paradigms: (i) model-based rewards, where a learned reward model predicts scalar or vector scores from synthetic and human feedback, and (ii) rule-based rewards, where domain-specific heuristics provide explicit correctness signals with confidence. Beyond accuracy, we further incorporate multi-aspect rewards to enforce instruction adherence and introduce a generalized length-penalty reward to stabilize training and improve performance. The proposed framework provides a flexible and effective approach to aligning MLLMs through reinforcement learning policy optimization. Our experiments show consistent improvements across different multimodal benchmarks when applying hybrid and multi-aspect reward modeling. Our best performing model in the 3B family achieves an overall average improvement of ~9.5% across general and math reasoning tasks. Focusing specifically on mathematical benchmarks, the model achieves a significant average improvement of ~16%, highlighting its effectiveness in mathematical reasoning and problem solving."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05283","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:20Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05271","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05271","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05271","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prashanth","familyName":"Arun","name":"Arun, Prashanth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vinita","familyName":"Vader","name":"Vader, Vinita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erya","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Erya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brent","familyName":"McCready-Branch","name":"McCready-Branch, Brent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sarah","familyName":"Seabrook","name":"Seabrook, Sarah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Scholz","name":"Scholz, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ana","familyName":"Crisan","name":"Crisan, Ana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Grossmann","name":"Grossmann, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pascal","familyName":"Poupart","name":"Poupart, Pascal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Chrysalis: A Unified System for Comparing Active Teaching and Passive Learning with AI Agents in Education"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:40:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"AI-assisted learning has seen a remarkable uptick over the last few years, mainly due to the rise in popularity of Large Language Models (LLMs). Their ability to hold long-form, natural language interactions with users makes them excellent resources for exploring school- and university-level topics in a dynamic, active manner. We compare students' experiences when interacting with an LLM companion in two capacities: tutored learning and learning-by-teaching. We do this using Chrysalis, an LLM-based system that we have designed to support both AI tutors and AI teachable agents for any topic. Through a within-subject exploratory study with 36 participants, we present insights into student preferences between the two strategies and how constructs such as intellectual humility vary between these two interaction modes. To our knowledge, we are the first to conduct a direct comparison study on the effects of using an LLM as a tutor versus as a teachable agent on multiple topics. We hope that our work opens up new avenues for future research in this area."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages. Submitted to EAAI-26 symposium. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05271","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Gui","name":"Gui, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Victor","familyName":"Veitch","name":"Veitch, Victor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lizhu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lizhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuokai","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhuokai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Let it Calm: Exploratory Annealed Decoding for Verifiable Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:15:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) is a powerful paradigm for enhancing the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs), yet its success hinges on effective exploration. An ideal exploration strategy must navigate two fundamental challenges: it must preserve sample quality while also ensuring training stability. While standard fixed-temperature sampling is simple, it struggles to balance these competing demands, as high temperatures degrade sample quality and low temperatures limit discovery. In this work, we propose a simpler and more effective strategy, Exploratory Annealed Decoding (EAD), grounded in the insight that exploration is most impactful on early tokens which define a sequence's semantic direction. EAD implements an intuitive **explore-at-the-beginning, exploit-at-the-end** strategy by annealing the sampling temperature from high to low during generation. This dynamic schedule encourages meaningful, high-level diversity at the start, then gradually lowers the temperature to preserve sample quality and keep the sampling distribution close to the target policy, which is essential for stable training. We demonstrate that EAD is a lightweight, plug-and-play method that significantly improves sample efficiency, consistently outperforming fixed-temperature sampling across various RLVR algorithms and model sizes. Our work suggests that aligning exploration with the natural dynamics of sequential generation offers a robust path to improving LLM reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Codebase: https://github.com/yangalan123/EAD-RLVR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:35Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:44:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05196","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05196","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05196","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daqian","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Daqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolei","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Xiaolei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinge","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jinge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Honghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiongfeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Xiongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Felix","familyName":"Naughton","name":"Naughton, Felix","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulina","familyName":"Bondaronek","name":"Bondaronek, Paulina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Graph-based LLM over Semi-Structured Population Data for Dynamic Policy Response"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:10:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Timely and accurate analysis of population-level data is crucial for effective decision-making during public health emergencies such as the COVID-19 pandemic. However, the massive input of semi-structured data, including structured demographic information and unstructured human feedback, poses significant challenges to conventional analysis methods. Manual expert-driven assessments, though accurate, are inefficient, while standard NLP pipelines often require large task-specific labeled datasets and struggle with generalization across diverse domains. To address these challenges, we propose a novel graph-based reasoning framework that integrates large language models with structured demographic attributes and unstructured public feedback in a weakly supervised pipeline. The proposed approach dynamically models evolving citizen needs into a need-aware graph, enabling population-specific analyses based on key features such as age, gender, and the Index of Multiple Deprivation. It generates interpretable insights to inform responsive health policy decision-making. We test our method using a real-world dataset, and preliminary experimental results demonstrate its feasibility. This approach offers a scalable solution for intelligent population health monitoring in resource-constrained clinical and governmental settings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Efficient Medical AI 2025 Workshop, MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05196","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:18Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05194","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05194","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05194","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anni","familyName":"Tziakouri","name":"Tziakouri, Anni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Menolascina","name":"Menolascina, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reinforcement Learning for Clinical Reasoning: Aligning LLMs with ACR Imaging Appropriateness Criteria"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:55:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:02:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Medical imaging has revolutionized diagnosis, yet unnecessary procedures are rising, exposing patients to radiation and stress, limiting equitable access, and straining healthcare systems. The American College of Radiology Appropriateness Criteria, developed through extensive multidisciplinary review, provide evidence-based guidance but remain underutilized. Leveraging advances in LLM reasoning, we introduce a Reasoning Agent trained with Reinforcement Learning (RL), specifically Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), to replicate expert clinical reasoning from the ACR Criteria. We present a novel RL approach for structured medical reasoning, systematically comparing reasoning-focused reward functions and evidence integration strategies. Our lightweight 8B model, MedReason-Embed, improves macro F1 by 18% over baseline, shows stronger reasoning alignment, and outperforms both larger and alternatively trained models, showing that reasoning-based supervision enables efficient, trustworthy clinical AI. Building on this, we design a modular end-to-end agentic architecture that automates imaging referrals: mapping diagnoses to ICD codes, retrieving PubMed evidence, and recommending optimal procedures. Crucially, the ability to generalize beyond static ACR guidelines not only enables clinicians to handle out-of-distribution cases, but also supports scaling the guideline development process itself, potentially reducing the significant effort required to create and update them. This work shows the potential of reasoning-focused RL within agentic architectures to deliver transparent, scalable, and reliable clinical decision support. Our code is available at: https://anonymous.4open.science/r/agentic-imaging-recommender-iclr-877D"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05194","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05187","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05187","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05187","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohamed","familyName":"El-Dosuky","name":"El-Dosuky, Mohamed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Real-time Framework for Interoperable Semantic-driven Internet-of-Things in Smart Agriculture"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:21:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Internet of Things (IoT) has revolutionized various applications including agriculture, but it still faces challenges in data collection and understanding. This paper proposes a real-time framework with three additional semantic layers to help IoT devices and sensors comprehend data meaning and source. The framework consists of six layers: perception, semantic annotation, interoperability, transportation, semantic reasoning, and application, suitable for dynamic environments. Sensors collect data in the form of voltage, which is then processed by microprocessors or microcontrollers in the semantic annotation and preprocessing layer. Metadata is added to the raw data, including the purpose, ID number, and application. Two semantic algorithms are proposed in the semantic interoperability and ontologies layer: the interoperability semantic algorithm for standardizing file types and the synonym identification algorithm for identifying synonyms. In the transportation layer, raw data and metadata are sent to other IoT devices or cloud computing platforms using techniques like WiFi, Zigbee networks, Bluetooth, and mobile communication networks. A semantic reasoning layer is proposed to infer new knowledge from the existing data, using fuzzy logic, Dempster-Shafer theory, and Bayesian networks. A Graphical User Interface (GUI) is proposed in the application layer to help users communicate with and monitor IoT sensors, devices, and new knowledge inferred. This framework provides a robust solution for managing IoT data, ensuring semantic completeness, and enabling real-time knowledge inference. The integration of uncertainty reasoning methods and semantic interoperability techniques makes this framework a valuable tool for advancing IoT applications in general and in agriculture in particular."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05187","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05185","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05185","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05185","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vrinda","familyName":"Malhotra","name":"Malhotra, Vrinda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaman","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nandini","familyName":"Pisupati","name":"Pisupati, Nandini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentZero++: Modeling Fear-Based Behavior"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T22:33:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present AgentZero++, an agent-based model that integrates cognitive, emotional, and social mechanisms to simulate decentralized collective violence in spatially distributed systems. Building on Epstein's Agent\\_Zero framework, we extend the original model with eight behavioral enhancements: age-based impulse control; memory-based risk estimation; affect-cognition coupling; endogenous destructive radius; fight-or-flight dynamics; affective homophily; retaliatory damage; and multi-agent coordination. These additions allow agents to adapt based on internal states, previous experiences, and social feedback, producing emergent dynamics such as protest asymmetries, escalation cycles, and localized retaliation. Implemented in Python using the Mesa ABM framework, AgentZero++ enables modular experimentation and visualization of how micro-level cognitive heterogeneity shapes macro-level conflict patterns. Our results highlight how small variations in memory, reactivity, and affective alignment can amplify or dampen unrest through feedback loops. By explicitly modeling emotional thresholds, identity-driven behavior, and adaptive networks, this work contributes a flexible and extensible platform for analyzing affective contagion and psychologically grounded collective action."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05185","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:03Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05169","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05169","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05169","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyu","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Guangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangzhe","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Xiangzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanxi","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hanxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Poisoned to Aware: Fostering Backdoor Self-Awareness in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:55:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) can acquire deceptive behaviors through backdoor attacks, where the model executes prohibited actions whenever secret triggers appear in the input. Existing safety training methods largely fail to address this vulnerability, due to the inherent difficulty of uncovering hidden triggers implanted in the model. Motivated by recent findings on LLMs' situational awareness, we propose a novel post-training framework that cultivates self-awareness of backdoor risks and enables models to articulate implanted triggers even when they are absent from the prompt. At its core, our approach introduces an inversion-inspired reinforcement learning framework that encourages models to introspectively reason about their own behaviors and reverse-engineer the triggers responsible for misaligned outputs. Guided by curated reward signals, this process transforms a poisoned model into one capable of precisely identifying its implanted trigger. Surprisingly, we observe that such backdoor self-awareness emerges abruptly within a short training window, resembling a phase transition in capability. Building on this emergent property, we further present two complementary defense strategies for mitigating and detecting backdoor threats. Experiments on five backdoor attacks, compared against six baseline methods, demonstrate that our approach has strong potential to improve the robustness of LLMs against backdoor risks. The code is available at LLM Backdoor Self-Awareness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05169","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:40Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05158","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05158","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05158","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"He","name":"He, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangliang","familyName":"You","name":"You, Liangliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongduan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Hongduan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ivor","familyName":"Tsang","name":"Tsang, Ivor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yew-Soon","familyName":"Ong","name":"Ong, Yew-Soon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lang-PINN: From Language to Physics-Informed Neural Networks via a Multi-Agent Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T08:20:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) provide a powerful approach for solving partial differential equations (PDEs), but constructing a usable PINN remains labor-intensive and error-prone. Scientists must interpret problems as PDE formulations, design architectures and loss functions, and implement stable training pipelines. Existing large language model (LLM) based approaches address isolated steps such as code generation or architecture suggestion, but typically assume a formal PDE is already specified and therefore lack an end-to-end perspective. We present Lang-PINN, an LLM-driven multi-agent system that builds trainable PINNs directly from natural language task descriptions. Lang-PINN coordinates four complementary agents: a PDE Agent that parses task descriptions into symbolic PDEs, a PINN Agent that selects architectures, a Code Agent that generates modular implementations, and a Feedback Agent that executes and diagnoses errors for iterative refinement. This design transforms informal task statements into executable and verifiable PINN code. Experiments show that Lang-PINN achieves substantially lower errors and greater robustness than competitive baselines: mean squared error (MSE) is reduced by up to 3--5 orders of magnitude, end-to-end execution success improves by more than 50\\%, and reduces time overhead by up to 74\\%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"PINN, PDE, Agent, LLM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05158","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:25Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05157","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05157","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05157","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abrar","familyName":"Shahid","name":"Shahid, Abrar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibteeker Mahir","familyName":"Ishum","name":"Ishum, Ibteeker Mahir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"AKM Tahmidul","familyName":"Haque","name":"Haque, AKM Tahmidul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M Sohel","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, M Sohel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. B. M. Alim Al","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, A. B. M. Alim Al","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adversarial Reinforcement Learning for Offensive and Defensive Agents in a Simulated Zero-Sum Network Environment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T05:53:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a controlled study of adversarial reinforcement learning in network security through a custom OpenAI Gym environment that models brute-force attacks and reactive defenses on multi-port services. The environment captures realistic security trade-offs including background traffic noise, progressive exploitation mechanics, IP-based evasion tactics, honeypot traps, and multi-level rate-limiting defenses. Competing attacker and defender agents are trained using Deep Q-Networks (DQN) within a zero-sum reward framework, where successful exploits yield large terminal rewards while incremental actions incur small costs. Through systematic evaluation across multiple configurations (varying trap detection probabilities, exploitation difficulty thresholds, and training regimens), the results demonstrate that defender observability and trap effectiveness create substantial barriers to successful attacks. The experiments reveal that reward shaping and careful training scheduling are critical for learning stability in this adversarial setting. The defender consistently maintains strategic advantage across 50,000+ training episodes, with performance gains amplifying when exposed to complex defensive strategies including adaptive IP blocking and port-specific controls. Complete implementation details, reproducible hyperparameter configurations, and architectural guidelines are provided to support future research in adversarial RL for cybersecurity. The zero-sum formulation and realistic operational constraints make this environment suitable for studying autonomous defense systems, attacker-defender co-evolution, and transfer learning to real-world network security scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 5 tables, 5 figures. 12th International Conference on Next Generation Computing, Communication, Systems and Security"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05157","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05156","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05156","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05156","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lesly","familyName":"Miculicich","name":"Miculicich, Lesly","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihir","familyName":"Parmar","name":"Parmar, Mihir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Palangi","name":"Palangi, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Krishnamurthy Dj","familyName":"Dvijotham","name":"Dvijotham, Krishnamurthy Dj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mirko","familyName":"Montanari","name":"Montanari, Mirko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomas","familyName":"Pfister","name":"Pfister, Tomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Long T.","familyName":"Le","name":"Le, Long T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VeriGuard: Enhancing LLM Agent Safety via Verified Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-03T04:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of autonomous AI agents in sensitive domains, such as healthcare, introduces critical risks to safety, security, and privacy. These agents may deviate from user objectives, violate data handling policies, or be compromised by adversarial attacks. Mitigating these dangers necessitates a mechanism to formally guarantee that an agent's actions adhere to predefined safety constraints, a challenge that existing systems do not fully address. We introduce VeriGuard, a novel framework that provides formal safety guarantees for LLM-based agents through a dual-stage architecture designed for robust and verifiable correctness. The initial offline stage involves a comprehensive validation process. It begins by clarifying user intent to establish precise safety specifications. VeriGuard then synthesizes a behavioral policy and subjects it to both testing and formal verification to prove its compliance with these specifications. This iterative process refines the policy until it is deemed correct. Subsequently, the second stage provides online action monitoring, where VeriGuard operates as a runtime monitor to validate each proposed agent action against the pre-verified policy before execution. This separation of the exhaustive offline validation from the lightweight online monitoring allows formal guarantees to be practically applied, providing a robust safeguard that substantially improves the trustworthiness of LLM agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05156","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05151","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05151","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05151","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prudence","familyName":"Djagba","name":"Djagba, Prudence","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelkader Y.","familyName":"Saley","name":"Saley, Abdelkader Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Large Language Models for Financial Applications: Techniques, Performance, and Challenges with FinMA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T11:19:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research explores the strengths and weaknesses of domain-adapted Large Language Models (LLMs) in the context of financial natural language processing (NLP). The analysis centers on FinMA, a model created within the PIXIU framework, which is evaluated for its performance in specialized financial tasks. Recognizing the critical demands of accuracy, reliability, and domain adaptation in financial applications, this study examines FinMA's model architecture, its instruction tuning process utilizing the Financial Instruction Tuning (FIT) dataset, and its evaluation under the FLARE benchmark. Findings indicate that FinMA performs well in sentiment analysis and classification, but faces notable challenges in tasks involving numerical reasoning, entity recognition, and summarization. This work aims to advance the understanding of how financial LLMs can be effectively designed and evaluated to assist in finance-related decision-making processes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05151","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:15Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05149","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05149","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05149","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Clarisse","familyName":"Sousa","name":"Sousa, Clarisse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiago","familyName":"Fonseca","name":"Fonseca, Tiago","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis Lino","familyName":"Ferreira","name":"Ferreira, Luis Lino","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo","familyName":"Venâncio","name":"Venâncio, Ricardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ricardo","familyName":"Severino","name":"Severino, Ricardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Percepta: High Performance Stream Processing at the Edge"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T08:57:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rise of real-time data and the proliferation of Internet of Things (IoT) devices have highlighted the limitations of cloud-centric solutions, particularly regarding latency, bandwidth, and privacy. These challenges have driven the growth of Edge Computing. Associated with IoT appears a set of other problems, like: data rate harmonization between multiple sources, protocol conversion, handling the loss of data and the integration with Artificial Intelligence (AI) models. This paper presents Percepta, a lightweight Data Stream Processing (DSP) system tailored to support AI workloads at the edge, with a particular focus on such as Reinforcement Learning (RL). It introduces specialized features such as reward function computation, data storage for model retraining, and real-time data preparation to support continuous decision-making. Additional functionalities include data normalization, harmonization across heterogeneous protocols and sampling rates, and robust handling of missing or incomplete data, making it well suited for the challenges of edge-based AI deployment."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05149","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Reinforcement Learning for Dynamic Configuration Allocation in Pre-Production Testing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T05:12:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring reliability in modern software systems requires rigorous pre-production testing across highly heterogeneous and evolving environments. Because exhaustive evaluation is infeasible, practitioners must decide how to allocate limited testing resources across configurations where failure probabilities may drift over time. Existing combinatorial optimization approaches are static, ad hoc, and poorly suited to such non-stationary settings. We introduce a novel reinforcement learning (RL) framework that recasts configuration allocation as a sequential decision-making problem. Our method is the first to integrate Q-learning with a hybrid reward design that fuses simulated outcomes and real-time feedback, enabling both sample efficiency and robustness. In addition, we develop an adaptive online-offline training scheme that allows the agent to quickly track abrupt probability shifts while maintaining long-run stability. Extensive simulation studies demonstrate that our approach consistently outperforms static and optimization-based baselines, approaching oracle performance. This work establishes RL as a powerful new paradigm for adaptive configuration allocation, advancing beyond traditional methods and offering broad applicability to dynamic testing and resource scheduling domains."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vanya Bannihatti","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Vanya Bannihatti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divyanshu","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Divyanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akhil","familyName":"Eppa","name":"Eppa, Akhil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neel","familyName":"Bhandari","name":"Bhandari, Neel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Curiosity-Driven LLM-as-a-judge for Personalized Creative Judgment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:29:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern large language models (LLMs) excel at objective tasks such as evaluating mathematical reasoning and factual accuracy, yet they falter when faced with the nuanced, subjective nature of assessing creativity. In this work, we propose a novel curiosity-driven LLM-as-a-judge for evaluating creative writing which is personlized to each individual's creative judgments. We use the Torrance Test of Creative Thinking(TTCW) benchmark introduced in Chakrabarty et al. (2024), which has stories annotated by expert humans across various subjective dimensions like Originality, to test our hypothesis. We show that our method enables models across various sizes, to learn the nuanced creative judgments of different individuals, by showing improvements over baseline supervised finetuning(SFT) method across various evaluation metrics like Pearson correlation, Cohen's and F1 values. Our method is especially useful in subjective evaluations where not all the annotators agree with each other."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:53Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:41:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Y.","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q.","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Q.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Characterizing Model Behavior Under Synthetic Data Training: An Empirical Study Across Scales and Mixing Ratios"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:28:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic data generated by large language models has become integral to modern NLP training pipelines, from bootstrapping reasoning capabilities to augmenting instruction-following datasets. While recent work demonstrates successful applications maintaining high external data ratios, systematic understanding of how synthetic data proportion affects model behavior across different scales remains limited. This paper presents a controlled empirical study examining model performance, calibration, and output characteristics when trained on varying synthetic-to-external data ratios. Using the Pythia model suite (410M-12B parameters) across five diverse tasks, we evaluate models after one to three training iterations with synthetic data proportions ranging from 0-50\\%. Our key findings include: models maintain stable performance with up to 20\\% synthetic data, but degradation accelerates beyond 30\\%; larger models (6.9B-12B) show greater robustness to synthetic data than smaller models (410M-1.4B); calibration degradation precedes accuracy loss, providing an early warning signal; and task characteristics matter, with reasoning tasks degrading faster than retrieval tasks under synthetic data training. Importantly, we find that current best practices, such as those employed in STaR and Self-Instruct systems that maintain greater than 80\\% external data, operate well within safe regimes identified by our experiments. We provide practical guidance for practitioners on synthetic data budgets based on model scale and task requirements, alongside detailed comparison with concurrent work including Shumailov et al.'s model collapse findings."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages. Technical report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:41:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Mingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bei","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengshuang","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hengshuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaya","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jiaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Noisy Traces to Stable Gradients: Bias-Variance Optimized Preference Optimization for Aligning Large Reasoning Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:58:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large reasoning models (LRMs) generate intermediate reasoning traces before producing final answers, yielding strong gains on multi-step and mathematical tasks. Yet aligning LRMs with human preferences, a crucial prerequisite for model deployment, remains underexplored. The statistically correct objective for preference alignment requires marginalizing over reasoning traces, but this computation is intractable in practice. A common workaround optimizes a single sampled trajectory, which introduces substantial gradient variance from stochastic trace sampling. To address this challenge, we frame preference optimization for LRMs through the lens of the bias--variance trade-off and propose Bias--Variance Optimized Preference Optimization (BVPO), a simple, drop-in method that mixes two gradient estimators: a high-variance trace-based estimator and a low-variance empty-trace estimator obtained by disabling reasoning trace generation. Our theory shows that BVPO strictly reduces trace-induced variance for any nontrivial mixture, provides a closed-form choice of the mixing weight that minimizes mean-squared error relative to the true marginal gradient, and under standard smoothness and step-size conditions, tightens classical convergence bounds for stochastic gradient descent. Empirically, BVPO improves alignment over the best baseline by up to 7.8 points on AlpacaEval~2 and 6.8 points on Arena-Hard. Despite being trained only on general conversational data, BVPO also boosts reasoning performance for base models by up to 4.0 points on the average of six math reasoning benchmarks. These results identify variance from trace sampling as a key bottleneck and demonstrate that directly optimizing the bias--variance trade-off yields more stable training and stronger overall performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:06:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:06:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:06:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreya","familyName":"Meel","name":"Meel, Shreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sennur","familyName":"Ulukus","name":"Ulukus, Sennur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Distributed Optimization With Feasible Set Privacy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:45:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We consider the problem of decentralized constrained optimization with multiple agents $E_1,\\ldots,E_N$ who jointly wish to learn the optimal solution set while keeping their feasible sets $\\mathcal{P}_1,\\ldots,\\mathcal{P}_N$ private from each other. We assume that the objective function $f$ is known to all agents and each feasible set is a collection of points from a universal alphabet $\\mathcal{P}_{alph}$. A designated agent (leader) starts the communication with the remaining (non-leader) agents, and is the first to retrieve the solution set. The leader searches for the solution by sending queries to and receiving answers from the non-leaders, such that the information on the individual feasible sets revealed to the leader should be no more than nominal, i.e., what is revealed from learning the solution set alone. We develop achievable schemes for obtaining the solution set at nominal information leakage, and characterize their communication costs under two communication setups between agents. In this work, we focus on two kinds of network setups: i) ring, where each agent communicates with two adjacent agents, and ii) star, where only the leader communicates with the remaining agents. We show that, if the leader first learns the joint feasible set through an existing private set intersection (PSI) protocol and then deduces the solution set, the information leaked to the leader is greater than nominal. Moreover, we draw connection of our schemes to threshold PSI (ThPSI), which is a PSI-variant where the intersection is revealed only when its cardinality is larger than a threshold value. Finally, for various realizations of $f$ mapped uniformly at random to a fixed range of values, our schemes are more communication-efficient with a high probability compared to retrieving the entire feasible set through PSI."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:04:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05059","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05059","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05059","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zach"],"givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Junlin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zach"],"givenName":"Jue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jue","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Zach"],"familyName":"Zhen","name":"Zhen","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"Xu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Athiwaratkun","name":"Athiwaratkun, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhuwan","familyName":"Dhingra","name":"Dhingra, Bhuwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ce","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Staircase Streaming for Low-Latency Multi-Agent Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:37:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:48:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) opened up new directions for leveraging the collective expertise of multiple LLMs. These methods, such as Mixture-of-Agents, typically employ additional inference steps to generate intermediate outputs, which are then used to produce the final response. While multi-agent inference can enhance response quality, it can significantly increase the time to first token (TTFT), posing a challenge for latency-sensitive applications and hurting user experience. To address this issue, we propose staircase streaming for low-latency multi-agent inference. Instead of waiting for the complete intermediate outputs from previous steps, we begin generating the final response as soon as we receive partial outputs from these steps. Experimental results demonstrate that staircase streaming reduces TTFT by up to 93% while maintaining response quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05059","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:04:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ondřej","familyName":"Kubíček","name":"Kubíček, Ondřej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viliam","familyName":"Lisý","name":"Lisý, Viliam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Look-ahead Reasoning with a Learned Model in Imperfect Information Games"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:26:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:47:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Test-time reasoning significantly enhances pre-trained AI agents' performance. However, it requires an explicit environment model, often unavailable or overly complex in real-world scenarios. While MuZero enables effective model learning for search in perfect information games, extending this paradigm to imperfect information games presents substantial challenges due to more nuanced look-ahead reasoning techniques and large number of states relevant for individual decisions. This paper introduces an algorithm LAMIR that learns an abstracted model of an imperfect information game directly from the agent-environment interaction. During test time, this trained model is used to perform look-ahead reasoning. The learned abstraction limits the size of each subgame to a manageable size, making theoretically principled look-ahead reasoning tractable even in games where previous methods could not scale. We empirically demonstrate that with sufficient capacity, LAMIR learns the exact underlying game structure, and with limited capacity, it still learns a valuable abstraction, which improves game playing performance of the pre-trained agents even in large games."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:04:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jihoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoyeon","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Hoyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun Kumar","familyName":"Chithanar","name":"Chithanar, Arun Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anit Kumar","familyName":"Sahu","name":"Sahu, Anit Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soummya","familyName":"Kar","name":"Kar, Soummya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chul","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Souradip","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Souradip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amrit Singh","familyName":"Bedi","name":"Bedi, Amrit Singh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Scaling in Diffusion LLMs via Hidden Semi-Autoregressive Experts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:16:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:47:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based large language models (dLLMs) are trained flexibly to model extreme dependence in the data distribution; however, how to best utilize this information at inference time remains an open problem. In this work, we uncover an interesting property of these models: dLLMs trained on textual data implicitly learn a mixture of semi-autoregressive experts, where different generation orders reveal different specialized behaviors. We show that committing to any single, fixed inference time schedule, a common practice, collapses performance by failing to leverage this latent ensemble. To address this, we introduce HEX (Hidden semiautoregressive EXperts for test-time scaling), a training-free inference method that ensembles across heterogeneous block schedules. By doing a majority vote over diverse block-sized generation paths, HEX robustly avoids failure modes associated with any single fixed schedule. On reasoning benchmarks such as GSM8K, it boosts accuracy by up to 3.56X (from 24.72% to 88.10%), outperforming top-K margin inference and specialized fine-tuned methods like GRPO, without additional training. HEX even yields significant gains on MATH benchmark from 16.40% to 40.00%, scientific reasoning on ARC-C from 54.18% to 87.80%, and TruthfulQA from 28.36% to 57.46%. Our results establish a new paradigm for test-time scaling in diffusion-based LLMs (dLLMs), revealing that the sequence in which masking is performed plays a critical role in determining performance during inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:03:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:03:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:03:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05010","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05010","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05010","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofía Perón","familyName":"Santana","name":"Santana, Sofía Perón","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ariel","familyName":"Fiuri","name":"Fiuri, Ariel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omar","familyName":"Osenda","name":"Osenda, Omar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martín","familyName":"Domínguez","name":"Domínguez, Martín","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimización de la Transmisión de Estados Cuánticos en Cadenas de Qubits usando Deep Reinforcement Learning y Algoritmos Genéticos"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:52:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantum state transfer (QST) via homogeneous spin chains plays a crucial role in building scalable quantum hardware. A basic quantum state transmission protocol prepares a state in one qubit and transfers it to another through a channel, seeking to minimize the time and avoid information loss. The fidelity of the process is measured by functions proportional to the transition probability between both states. We approach this optimization problem using constant magnetic pulses and two complementary strategies: deep reinforcement learning, where an agent learns pulse sequences through rewards, and genetic algorithms, which develop candidate solutions through selection and mutation. We analyze the efficiency of both methods and their ability to incorporate physical constraints."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"in Spanish language"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05010","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:02:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05003","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05003","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05003","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Mansha","name":"Mansha, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resource-Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLaMA-3.2-3B for Medical Chain-of-Thought Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:42:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT-4 and LLaMA have demonstrated remarkable reasoning abilities but require significant computational resources for fine-tuning. This paper presents a resource-efficient fine-tuning approach for LLaMA-3.2-3B to enhance medical chain-of-thought reasoning while operating under constrained GPU and memory settings. Using parameter-efficient tuning techniques such as LoRA and QLoRA, we adapt the base model on publicly available medical reasoning datasets. The model achieves improved reasoning coherence and factual accuracy while reducing memory usage by up to 60% compared to standard full fine-tuning. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that lightweight adaptations can retain strong reasoning capability in medical question-answering tasks. This work highlights practical strategies for deploying LLMs in low-resource research environments and provides insights into balancing efficiency and domain specialization for medical AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 2 figures. Submitted to arXiv for open access"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05003","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:02:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aditya","familyName":"Sripada","name":"Sripada, Aditya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek","familyName":"Warrier","name":"Warrier, Abhishek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Walking, Rolling, and Beyond: First-Principles and RL Locomotion on a TARS-Inspired Robot"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:39:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic locomotion research typically draws from biologically inspired leg designs, yet many human-engineered settings can benefit from non-anthropomorphic forms. TARS3D translates the block-shaped 'TARS' robot from Interstellar into a 0.25 m, 0.99 kg research platform with seven actuated degrees of freedom. The film shows two primary gaits: a bipedal-like walk and a high-speed rolling mode. For TARS3D, we build reduced-order models for each, derive closed-form limit-cycle conditions, and validate the predictions on hardware. Experiments confirm that the robot respects its +/-150 degree hip limits, alternates left-right contacts without interference, and maintains an eight-step hybrid limit cycle in rolling mode. Because each telescopic leg provides four contact corners, the rolling gait is modeled as an eight-spoke double rimless wheel. The robot's telescopic leg redundancy implies a far richer gait repertoire than the two limit cycles treated analytically. So, we used deep reinforcement learning (DRL) in simulation to search the unexplored space. We observed that the learned policy can recover the analytic gaits under the right priors and discover novel behaviors as well. Our findings show that TARS3D's fiction-inspired bio-transcending morphology can realize multiple previously unexplored locomotion modes and that further learning-driven search is likely to reveal more. This combination of analytic synthesis and reinforcement learning opens a promising pathway for multimodal robotics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 10 figures. Presented at IEEE-RAS International Conference on Humanoid Robots (Humanoids) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:02:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04980","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04980","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04980","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangzhou","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Fangzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunkit","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Chunkit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yauwai","familyName":"Yim","name":"Yim, Yauwai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangqiu","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yangqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Hanabi: Evaluating Multi-Agent Gameplays with Theory-of-Mind and Rationale Inference in Imperfect Information Collaboration Game"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:17:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:44:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective multi-agent collaboration requires agents to infer the rationale behind others' actions, a capability rooted in Theory-of-Mind (ToM). While recent Large Language Models (LLMs) excel at logical inference, their ability to infer rationale in dynamic, collaborative settings remains under-explored. This study introduces LLM-Hanabi, a novel benchmark that uses the cooperative game Hanabi to evaluate the rationale inference and ToM of LLMs. Our framework features an automated evaluation system that measures both game performance and ToM proficiency. Across a range of models, we find a significant positive correlation between ToM and in-game success. Notably, first-order ToM (interpreting others' intent) correlates more strongly with performance than second-order ToM (predicting others' interpretations). These findings highlight that for effective AI collaboration, the ability to accurately interpret a partner's rationale is more critical than higher-order reasoning. We conclude that prioritizing first-order ToM is a promising direction for enhancing the collaborative capabilities of future models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Wordplay"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04980","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:01:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satrio","familyName":"Pambudi","name":"Pambudi, Satrio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filippo","familyName":"Menolascina","name":"Menolascina, Filippo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Clinical Narratives and ACR Appropriateness Guidelines: A Multi-Agent RAG System for Medical Imaging Decisions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantitative Methods (q-bio.QM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:04:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:43:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The selection of appropriate medical imaging procedures is a critical and complex clinical decision, guided by extensive evidence-based standards such as the ACR Appropriateness Criteria (ACR-AC). However, the underutilization of these guidelines, stemming from the difficulty of mapping unstructured patient narratives to structured criteria, contributes to suboptimal patient outcomes and increased healthcare costs. To bridge this gap, we introduce a multi-agent cognitive architecture that automates the translation of free-text clinical scenarios into specific, guideline-adherent imaging recommendations. Our system leverages a novel, domain-adapted dense retrieval model, ColBERT, fine-tuned on a synthetically generated dataset of 8,840 clinical scenario-recommendation pairs to achieve highly accurate information retrieval from the ACR-AC knowledge base. This retriever identifies candidate guidelines with a 93.9% top-10 recall, which are then processed by a sequence of LLM-based agents for selection and evidence-based synthesis. We evaluate our architecture using GPT-4.1 and MedGemma agents, demonstrating a state-of-the-art exact match accuracy of 81%, meaning that in 81% of test cases the predicted procedure set was identical to the guideline's reference set, and an F1-score of 0.879. This represents a 67-percentage-point absolute improvement in accuracy over a strong standalone GPT-4.1 baseline, underscoring the contribution that our architecture makes to a frontier model. These results were obtained on a challenging test set with substantial lexical divergence from the source guidelines. Our code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/demo-iclr-B567/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:00:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:00:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:00:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04934","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04934","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04934","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satvik","familyName":"Dixit","name":"Dixit, Satvik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soham","familyName":"Deshmukh","name":"Deshmukh, Soham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bhiksha","familyName":"Raj","name":"Raj, Bhiksha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AURA Score: A Metric For Holistic Audio Question Answering Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:41:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Audio Question Answering (AQA) is a key task for evaluating Audio-Language Models (ALMs), yet assessing open-ended responses remains challenging. Existing metrics used for AQA such as BLEU, METEOR and BERTScore, mostly adapted from NLP and audio captioning, rely on surface similarity and fail to account for question context, reasoning, and partial correctness. To address the gap in literature, we make three contributions in this work. First, we introduce AQEval to enable systematic benchmarking of AQA metrics. It is the first benchmark of its kind, consisting of 10k model responses annotated by multiple humans for their correctness and relevance. Second, we conduct a comprehensive analysis of existing AQA metrics on AQEval, highlighting weak correlation with human judgment, especially for longer answers. Third, we propose a new metric - AURA score, to better evaluate open-ended model responses. On AQEval, AURA achieves state-of-the-art correlation with human ratings, significantly outperforming all baselines. Through this work, we aim to highlight the limitations of current AQA evaluation methods and motivate better metrics. We release both the AQEval benchmark and the AURA metric to support future research in holistic AQA evaluation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04934","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:59:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:59:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:59:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04901","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04901","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04901","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan Colaço","familyName":"Carr","name":"Carr, Jonathan Colaço","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Qinyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cameron","familyName":"Allen","name":"Allen, Cameron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Focused Skill Discovery: Learning to Control Specific State Variables while Minimizing Side Effects"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:17:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Skills are essential for unlocking higher levels of problem solving. A common approach to discovering these skills is to learn ones that reliably reach different states, thus empowering the agent to control its environment. However, existing skill discovery algorithms often overlook the natural state variables present in many reinforcement learning problems, meaning that the discovered skills lack control of specific state variables. This can significantly hamper exploration efficiency, make skills more challenging to learn with, and lead to negative side effects in downstream tasks when the goal is under-specified. We introduce a general method that enables these skill discovery algorithms to learn focused skills -- skills that target and control specific state variables. Our approach improves state space coverage by a factor of three, unlocks new learning capabilities, and automatically avoids negative side effects in downstream tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Reinforcement Learning Journal 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04901","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:58:01Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:58:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:58:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":207,"totalPages":3,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22+OR+reasoning+OR+%22reinforcement+learning%22%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026sort=registered","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28agent+OR+agents+OR+%22tool+calling%22+OR+%22tool+use%22+OR+%22retrieval+augmented%22+OR+%22retrieval-augmented%22+OR+reasoning+OR+%22reinforcement+learning%22%29"}}